\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar drifts down vs yen as BOJ stays out. \b0\par\par\uc2 739 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 1999\par 09:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 22 (Reuters) - The dollar was lower against the yen by late afternoon in Tokyo on Monday as the Bank of Japan's absence from the market eroded the greenback's early gains. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar was still supported by speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could step in to check the yen's rise if the greenback dips sharply below 122 yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 121.95/00 yen as of 0606 GMT, compared with 122.30/35 yen in late New York on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose to 122.50 yen earlier, but offers by Japanese exporters prevented the dollar from rising further, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Without the prodding of Japanese (monetary) authorities, it is hard for the dollar to stay above 122 yen. Left alone, the yen will probably appreciate," said a city bank dealer, noting that dollar-selling interest among Japanese exporters was strong, particularly above 122.50 yen. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they were still wary about the possibility of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara said on Tuesday that a weaker yen was acceptable if needed for Japan's economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "If the markets think that a weaker yen is necessary for an economic recovery, we will accept that. The most important thing right now is an economic recovery," Sakakibara said. \par \par \uc2  Separately, Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a message to the markets that a sharp rise in the yen based on expectations of a quick Japanese economic recovery would be disruptive. But dealers said the impact of recent verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Sakakibara had been somewhat weakened by a cool response from European and the U.S. authorities and signs that some Asian countries may be concerned about the recent weakening of the yen. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara told reporters late on Monday that the authorities in Japan, the United states and Europe will closely monitor \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates and cooperate with each other. \par \par \uc2  But Bundesbank president-designate Ernst Welteke said on Monday that the ECB's euro-buying, yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Friday on behalf of the BOJ did not imply that it had any material interest in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin said in a television interview: "I don't think any country should use its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an instrument of trade policy." \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, an official at South Korea's Finance Ministry said the Seoul government was closely watching dollar/yen's impact on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and was also concerned about the won's strength against the yen. \par \par \uc2  South Korea is concerned the won's appreciation against the yen will hurt its export competitiveness and its nascent economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "Sakakibara's comments on Monday that Japanese authorities do not intend to contain \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates within a narrow range fuelled some speculation that Japan may allow the yen to fall to, say, 130 to the dollar," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  But political pressure from foreign countries and concerns about the impact of a sharp yen depreciation on Asian economies may prevent such falls in the yen, the dealer said. The yen also rose against the euro on Tuesday, standing at 125.86/94 yen compared with 126.62 yen in late New York. \par \par \uc2  The euro was hurt by comments made by European Commission President-designate Romano Prodi overnight, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Prodi said it would be difficult for Italy to remain in the euro if its inflation rate remained out of line with its single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  partners in the long term. \par \par \uc2  He later clarified those remarks, saying that there was no risk of Italy leaving the euro and that his remarks were reported "in a very ambiguous and wrong way." \par \par \uc2  The euro stood at $1.0324/26 at 0653 GMT, compared with $1.0348/55 in late New York on Monday. But dealers were wary about selling the euro for yen aggressively following the BOJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euro against yen via the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers speculated that Japanese authorities want to see the euro above 130 yen to help investors and exporters. \par \par \uc2  "A level of 130 yen to the euro has been used by some Japanese exporters in drawing up their business plans," the city bank dealer said. ((Masayuki Kitano, Tokyo Treasury Desk 81-3-3432-9780 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6m0604f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
