\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW-Krugman says US bubble a risk to world. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Swaha Pattanaik \par \uc2 608 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 1999\par 18:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 22 (Reuters) - Japan and Southeast Asian economies are not yet out of the woods but it is the U.S. economy which poses the biggest risk to world growth, influential U.S. economist Paul Krugman said on Friday. "If you are looking for the world's biggest risk, I would probably put it in my home country - if you are looking for a bubble that can burst, it's back in the U.S.," Krugman, economics professor at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, told Reuters in an interview. \par \par \uc2  "I have been expecting it every month for the past several years but the bubble just keeps on getting bigger." \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones Industrial Average is up 17 percent in the year to date and more than 50 percent higher than in December 1996 when U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan warned of "irrational exuberance" in the stock market. \par \par \uc2  Krugman said it was difficult to pinpoint what would trigger a major reversal in the U.S. stock market and that it could be any one of several possibilities, including higher-than-expected inflation numbers. \par \par \uc2  Turning to Asia, Krugman said the unexpected strength of Japan's economic expansion in the first quarter had not convinced him that the long-stagnant economy had turned the corner. \par \par \uc2  "When the news sounds too good to be true, it probably is," he said. "I don't think anything fundamental has changed in the Japanese situation." \par \par \uc2  He added that Japan's efforts to prevent a rise in the yen which could snuff out economic recovery were doomed to failure unless its monetary authorities used more unconventional means to curb demand for yen than they had used so far. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the foreign exchanges on four days in the past two weeks, even channelling sales of yen through the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  However, the impact of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been somewhat diluted as it has mopped up the extra yen that has flooded into the market via its money-market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a process known as sterlisation. \par \par \uc2  "If people don't think they are going to allow the monetary base to expand very rapidly, if they think this is just an attempt to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets without a real change elsewhere, then it will be blown away by the markets the way that sterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  always are," Krugman said. \par \par \uc2  "For those of us that think that what Japan needs to do is to roll the printing presses, and to engage in unconventional monetary expansion, one readily available form of unconventional monetary expansion is unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Krugman said he was also skeptical about whether the performance of the southeast Asian economies justified some of the confidence that investors and analysts were beginning to show in the region. \par \par \uc2  "The good times are not back," he said. "They are probably past the worst of it but I don't think they are out of the woods at all yet." \par \par \uc2  In Europe, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched at the start of the year was "in some sense sold under false pretences" and was not about to recoup any significant part of the 13 percent it has lost against the dollar since its inception. \par \par \uc2  "In the long run probably the euro is currently undervalued against the U.S. dollar but in the long run we are all dead and I don't see any turnaround anytime soon," he said. \par \par \uc2  ((Swaha Pattanaik, London Capital Markets, +44 171 542 6284, swaha.pattanaik@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6m05ycy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
