\par\par \b \uc2  Fear of Japanese  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  haunts yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 597 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 1999\par 17:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 22 (Reuters) - The yen was largely immobilised against the dollar and euro in Europe on Tuesday as the threat of further Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength hung over dealers. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  stayed out of the market on Tuesday, after repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday and Monday, but further yen sales were likely, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "We had a break from the BOJ last night as they like to avoid being too predictable, but there is no way we've seen the last of it," said Ian Gunner, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro. \par \par \uc2  The yen strengthened a little, to trade at 121.75/80 per dollar and 125.59/55 per euro at 1437 GMT, from 122.14/24 and 126.08/20 respectively in late European trade on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  But more direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was possible on Wednesday, analysts said, after verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Tokyo on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Senior Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Eisuke Sakakibara repeated his recent remarks that a weaker yen was acceptable if it was needed for the nation's economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa, in contrast, said the recent BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were aimed at pre-empting an unjustified sharp rise in the yen, not at boosting the economy. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday with the dollar as high as 122 yen made it hard for dealers to predict the levels in either dollar/yen or euro/yen at which the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to act next. \par \par \uc2  "When the Bank of Japan sees a risk that the dollar could break out to the downside of its 115-125 yen range, it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  strongly," said Daniel Moreno, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Benson in Frankfurt. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen has been trading above 115 yen since mid-February. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said a depreciation in the yen against the euro would also suit both the BOJ and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which on Friday said it bought euros for yen for the BOJ. \par \par \uc2  "We see euro/yen at 130-135 by the end of the year," said Moreno. \par \par \uc2  The euro was caught in a half-cent range above $1.03 on Tuesday, still suffering after comments from European Commission President-designate Romano Prodi on Monday on Italy's place in European economic and monetary union. \par \par \uc2  Prodi later that day clarified his earlier remarks, saying that there was no risk of Italy bowing out of the euro. \par \par \uc2  But the negative impact on the euro showed the vulnerability of Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The euro is a wounded animal, and the bias is still to the downside," said Gunner. \par \par \uc2  The euro was trading at the week's lows at $1.0302/04 at 1457 GMT, from $1.0320/25 in late European trade on Tuesday and from highs of $1.0420 set on Monday, prior to Prodi's comments. \par \par \uc2  But the euro was likely to be supported around $1.0250-60 life lows set on June 7 while the Bank of Japan put a safety net below euro/yen losses, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The pound gained slightly against dollar and euro after Britain's Trade and Industry Minister Stephen Byers said Britain was unlikely to meet the economic tests required for euro membership before 2001 at the earliest and would not join for "a few years yet". \par \par \uc2  It was trading at $1.5878/87 and 64.85/93 pence per euro, from $1.5882/87 and 64.98/01 pence late in Europe on Monday. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6m05y5y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
