\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Dollar tiptoes lower vs yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,204 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 1999\par 13:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 22 (Reuters) - The dollar tiptoed lower against the yen in European business on Tuesday, giving up some of its recent gains in the absence of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The euro drifted in a tight range in listless trading. \par \par \uc2  The dollar eroded toward 121.50 yen as Monday's rally ignited by aggressive BOJ buying ran out of steam overnight in the 122.50 area - the highs from early June and Monday as well. \par \par \uc2  "I think it's fairly clear that the bottom of the range has been brought higher (by BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ), but it's still quite disappointing that we haven't seen a more fruitful rally," said Jane Foley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital in London. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 121.56/61 yen at 1045 GMT, down from 122.14/24 late in Monday's European session. \par \par \uc2  Still, the market remained extremely jumpy about BOJ action. \par \par \uc2  Early in European trading, the dollar suddenly jumped by a quarter yen to slightly above 122. The greenback soon resumed its descent as no BOJ presence was detected, although the South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was buying dollars for won at that time. \par \par \uc2  Traders said there was little doubt that the BOJ would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again if the dollar kept sliding. \par \par \uc2  "Every time they said they would, they have," said a dealer at a European bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Overnight, the Japanese Finance Ministry's Eisuke "Mr Yen" Sakakibara repeated his recent remarks that a weaker yen was acceptable if it was needed for the nation's economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa, on the other hand, said the recent BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were aimed at pre-empting an unjustified sharp rise in the yen, not at boosting the economy. \par \par \uc2  Foley at Barclays observed that in order for the dollar to sustain a rally on its own, the U.S. Treasury market had to stabilise and allay Japanese investors' risk concerns. \par \par \uc2  "What's crucial for the Treasury market will be the FOMC (Federal Open Market Committee meeting, June 29-30)," she said. \par \par \uc2  The euro consolidated in a tight half-cent range above $1.03 after Monday's slide on comment by European Commission President-designate Romano Prodi on Italy's place in European economic and monetary union. \par \par \uc2  Prodi later that day clarified his earlier remarks, saying that there was no risk of Italy bowing out of the euro. \par \par \uc2  A European Commission spokesman said on Tuesday Italy's inflation "in itself" was not a major worrying factor and that the EU treaty was clear on the irrevocable nature of the union. \par \par \uc2  The euro stood at $1.0326/30 at 1105 GMT versus $1.0320/25 in Monday's late European session. \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen was near the day's low, moving in tandem with dollar/yen's decline. It was down to 125.65/75 yen from late Monday's 126.08/20. \par \par \uc2  Foley said the market was still awaiting confirmation that the European economy was picking up. \par \par \uc2  "We still have got credibility problems associated with too many officials saying too many different things, and we're still not seeing enough structural reforms," she added. \par \par \uc2  The German Finance Ministry said on Tuesday it saw growing signs of economic recovery and a favourable inflation environment, but no improvement in the labour market. \par \par \uc2  France's industrial output was down 0.6 percent in April, while March data were revised higher. \par \par \uc2  Bank of France Governor Jean-Claude Trichet speaks on the euro in Geneva at 1630 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Sterling bounced off its early lows against the dollar and euro, taking heart from remarks by UK Trade and Industry Secretary Stephen Byers that Britain was unlikely to meet economic tests for euro entry until 2001 at the earliest. \par \par \uc2  The pound stood at $1.5874/84 at 1144 GMT, virtually unchanged from late in Monday's European session, while euro/sterling was at 65.01/03 pence against 64.98/01. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 121.56/61 yen at 1045 GMT, down from 122.14/24 late in Monday's European session. \par \par \uc2  Still, the market remained extremely jumpy about BOJ action. \par \par \uc2  Early in European trading, the dollar suddenly jumped by a quarter yen to slightly above 122. The greenback soon resumed its descent as no BOJ presence was detected, although the South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was buying dollars for won at that time. \par \par \uc2  Traders said there was little doubt that the BOJ would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again if the dollar kept sliding. \par \par \uc2  "Every time they said they would, they have," said a dealer at a European bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Overnight, the Japanese Finance Ministry's Eisuke "Mr Yen" Sakakibara repeated his recent remarks that a weaker yen was acceptable if it was needed for the nation's economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa, on the other hand, said the recent BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were aimed at pre-empting an unjustified sharp rise in the yen, not at boosting the economy. \par \par \uc2  Foley at Barclays observed that in order for the dollar to sustain a rally on its own, the U.S. Treasury market had to stabilise and allay Japanese investors' risk concerns. \par \par \uc2  "What's crucial for the Treasury market will be the FOMC (Federal Open Market Committee meeting, June 29-30)," she said. \par \par \uc2  The euro consolidated in a tight half-cent range above $1.03 after Monday's slide on comment by European Commission President-designate Romano Prodi on Italy's place in European economic and monetary union. \par \par \uc2  Prodi later that day clarified his earlier remarks, saying that there was no risk of Italy bowing out of the euro. \par \par \uc2  A European Commission spokesman said on Tuesday Italy's inflation "in itself" was not a major worrying factor and that the EU treaty was clear on the irrevocable nature of the union. \par \par \uc2  The euro stood at $1.0326/30 at 1105 GMT versus $1.0320/25 in Monday's late European session. \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen was near the day's low, moving in tandem with dollar/yen's decline. It was down to 125.65/75 yen from late Monday's 126.08/20. \par \par \uc2  Foley said the market was still awaiting confirmation that the European economy was picking up. \par \par \uc2  "We still have got credibility problems associated with too many officials saying too many different things, and we're still not seeing enough structural reforms," she added. \par \par \uc2  The German Finance Ministry said on Tuesday it saw growing signs of economic recovery and a favourable inflation environment, but no improvement in the labour market. \par \par \uc2  France's industrial output was down 0.6 percent in April, while March data were revised higher. \par \par \uc2  Bank of France Governor Jean-Claude Trichet speaks on the euro in Geneva at 1630 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Sterling bounced off its early lows against the dollar and euro, taking heart from remarks by UK Trade and Industry Secretary Stephen Byers that Britain was unlikely to meet economic tests for euro entry until 2001 at the earliest. \par \par \uc2  The pound stood at $1.5874/84 at 1144 GMT, virtually unchanged from late in Monday's European session, while euro/sterling was