\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of Japan Fighting To Hold Yen Bulls To Comfort Zone \b0\par\par\uc2  By Marianne Sullivan \par \uc2 802 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 1999\par 20:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Eager investors aren't making it easy for the Bank of Japan to keep the dollar in the Y120.00 to Y125.00 range, which analysts have pinpointed as its comfort zone. \par \par \uc2  In the last three weeks, yen-buying interest, fueled by hopes that Japan's economy is on the verge of a turnaround, pushed the yen about 5.7% higher against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  But the BOJ is now showing yen enthusiasts it's ready to do battle, pulling out all the stops and pouring some $20 billion into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to prevent yen appreciation from foiling the country's efforts at economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Since June 10, the BOJ has made four separate incursions into \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets - three times to slow yen gains on the dollar and once through the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to block a similar rise against the euro. \par \par \uc2  Backing up the BOJ's Monday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with some verbal reminders, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Vice Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara reconfirmed: "At this stage, a premature strengthening of the yen is undesirable. We have an extremely strong will to stop this. If needed, we are ready to take decisive action," \par \par \uc2  In fact, one reason the BOJ can even consider taking on such a powerful interest in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is that the fundamentals don't yet support a stronger yen, said Marc Chandler, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Mellon Bank in Pittsburgh. \par \par \uc2  The yen began this appreciation May 20, after the dollar topped out at Y124.70, its highest level since October 1998. \par \par \uc2  The yen's rise came amid a flurry of foreign buying interest in Japan's Nikkei 225 stock index, which had risen about 25% on year. But the run up in the stock market wasn't accompanied by other signs of a turnaround and the stronger yen hurts exporters who have been a major engine of Japan's growth revival. \par \par \uc2  In fact, after its prolonged recession, Japan got its first sign of hope only June 10 when data showed the economy had grown 1.9% in the first quarter. It was that same day, when the dollar traded as low as Y117.63, the BOJ made its first move. \par \par \uc2  In addition to spending a total of roughly $20 billion in an attempt to convince \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets it is serious about keeping the yen weak, the BOJ also enlisted the support of the ECB. To further drive home its message, it has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  not just to stop the yen's appreciation against the dollar but against the euro as well. \par \par \uc2  Despite the BOJ's obvious commitment to the yen's weaker range, investors seem equally determined to get a piece of Japan's nascent recovery. Monday, the same day, the BOJ was conducting its fourth assault on yen strength Japan's Nikkei 225 stock index rose 1.77%, rallying to 17,738.85, its highest level since Oct. 6, 1999. \par \par \uc2  Chandler said it will be tough for the BOJ to maintain such a tight trading range for very long, but it might buy Japan enough time to allow the economy to catch up with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be sustained with in the face of such buying interest depends on two factors, Chandler said: "Is it large enough and is it backed up by fundamentals?" \par \par \uc2  Analysts agree the yen's current strength is dictated by short-term considerations. In addition to foreign investment in Japanese stocks, Japanese investors are also bringing money home to invest in their improving stock market. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ is trying to counter these short-term considerations and that is why it can work." Chandler said. \par \par \uc2  But, Kathleen Lino, a fund manager at Avatar Associates in New York, said her own fund, whose benchmark for Japan is 14% of an equities portfolio which makes up 60% of its total portfolio, is planning to increase its exposure. Her fund had been as much as 10% overweight Japan in March and April and then scaled back to the current position. \par \par \uc2  "Our target is to increase another 4%," Lino said. We want to see how the market does," \par \par \uc2  Lino said the positive first quarter GDP numbers were the first positive sign from the country in two years, but reminded it was only one piece of data. \par \par \uc2  "There have been a lot of false starts before. The issue is whether Japan can implement stabilizing policies and if it can achieve longer term growth" \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One indication that this stock rally may be a little more long lasting\par than in March and April is that local investors have joined in, Lino\par said. Last time, it was led by foreigners.\par -By Marianne Sullivan; 201-938-4385\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 21-06-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1856GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1140 4218 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv6l04q9q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
