\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Asia: BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Aims Largely At Boosting Euro \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Holland \par \uc2 890 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 1999\par 07:37\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the last two weeks are finally beginning to convince the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to revise its medium-term directional outlook for the yen. \par \par \uc2  Although Japanese corporations and both Japanese and foreign institutional investors are still queuing up to buy the yen against the dollar and euro, shorter-term market players are coming around to the view that the BOJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may succeed in forcing the U.S. and European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  significantly higher against the yen. \par \par \uc2  After buying euros against yen on Friday, the BOJ was back in the market first thing Monday morning, this time buying U.S. dollars against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's action has now pushed the dollar up by over 4% from its June 11 low against the yen of Y117.65. The euro is up by around 3.7% from Friday's low of Y122.47. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan is fighting an uphill battle, however. Both trade and investment flows are running in the yen's favor, turning the BOJ into what the head trader at one Japanese bank in Singapore describes as the "dollar and euro buyer of last resort". \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Yen Supported By Institutional Buying\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It's not only Japan's trade surplus with the rest of the world -\par logged at Y834.34 billion or $6.84 billion in May - that is supporting\par the yen. Encouraged by unexpectedly strong first-quarter GDP figures\par showing an annualized 7.9% rate of economic growth, foreign investors\par have stepped up their buying of Japanese equities, driving the benchmark\par Nikkei 225 stock average to its highest level for 1999 on Monday\par morning.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "At this point the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  doesn't seem to be convincing the\par market that the dollar has found a solid base against the yen. The\par investor community is still looking to sell the dollar on upticks," says\par Ron Leven, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at J.P. Morgan in Singapore.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But trade and investment inflows into Japan are only half the story\par behind the yen's strength. Equally important, or even more important\par according to some observers, are the dynamics of the euro/yen market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It all boils down to the euro/yen," says Chia Woon Khien, treasury\par economist at Skandinaviska Enskilda Banken in Singapore, arguing that\par the BOJ's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are aimed at relieving some of the pressure on\par Japanese investors, who are large holders of the euro.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Japanese insurers and pension funds, and even individual investors,\par were big buyers of the Deutschemark last year, betting on a strong\par euro," explains the Japanese bank trading chief, adding: "But the euro\par has been a big disappointment."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par From its late February high of Y135.00, the euro had declined by more\par than 9% against the yen before being boosted by the Bank of Japan on\par Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For Japanese investors who bought heavily into European assets toward\par the end of 1998 and in the early months of 1999 at levels equivalent to\par Y135 or even Y140 to the euro, that slide represents a heavy loss to add\par to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hits sustained in the dollar/yen market during 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    BOJ Seen Assisting Institutional Holders Of Euro\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In recent weeks Japanese investors have emerged as big sellers of\par euros against the yen, as they attempt to hedge their underlying\par positions in euro assets. But hedging against further euro losses has\par only driven the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lower, exacerbating the losses for the\par large numbers of Japanese investors who remain unhedged.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For the unhedged, the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has come as a\par lifeline. According to some analysts, the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par anxious to limit investor losses, has prodded the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  into\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to depress the yen and support the euro, in an attempt to\par drive the euro back up to levels around Y130 or Y135.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Until the euro gets back up to those levels, I don't think the Bank\par of Japan will give up," says Chia at Enskilda.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par An increasing number of short-term traders are now buying into Chia's\par view, establishing long positions in the dollar and euro against the yen\par in the hope of getting a free ride from the Bank of Japan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "If you want to trade, and you're not already sitting on a position,\par you should get on the long side of dollar/yen and let the Bank of Japan\par make you some money," advises a trader at a German bank in Singapore.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Given the supply and demand equation, I think we will continue to see\par the Bank of Japan on the bid for dollars and euros for the foreseeable\par future," says one Japanese bank trader, adding that he is buying euros.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "But in the longer time frame, if the first-quarter GDP numbers were\par not just a flash in the pan, I feel the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will fail and that\par the yen will continue to appreciate," he cautions.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Chia agrees, saying: "At some stage we are going to see a very sharp\par reversal in the yen. By the end of September, I think we could see the\par dollar back below Y118."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Tom Holland; 852 2832 2341; tom.holland@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 21-06-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0537GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4258 5095 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv6l04nvc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
