\par\par \b \uc2  = \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Accounts Stay Cautious On Tsys Amid  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonoko Setaishi \par \uc2 691 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 1999\par 23:13\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Despite the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's\par resolve to weaken its own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in favor of the dollar,\par Japanese investors aren't exactly snapping up Treasurys.\par    A stronger dollar makes Treasurys more attractive to\par overseas investors by promising windfall \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gains when\par the proceeds are repatriated into their own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par    And the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appears determined to keep\par the yen weak, after stepping into the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par market four times in less than two weeks to support the\par dollar and the euro against the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par    But skepticism over the BOJ's long-term commitment to a\par weaker yen is keeping most Japanese investors on the\par sidelines, traders and sales managers said.\par    "Everyone agrees that the Bank of Japan is simply buying\par time" for its moribund economy to get back on a solid\par recovery path, said one New York-based sales manager at a\par major Japanese bank.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    "The economy has barely stopped sinking but is still on\par a very fragile footing - and you don't leave the buds out in\par the wind at a time like this," he added.\par    A weaker yen helps the Japanese economy by aiding its\par huge export industry.\par    And some Japanese investors believe the BOJ will end its\par vigilance and allow the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to strengthen before long,\par traders said.\par    "Do they think this is the beginning of a long-lasting\par trend toward a weak yen? The answer is no," the sales\par manager said.\par    In addition, Japanese accounts are worried that the\par closely watched "tankan" survey of business sentiment -\par slated for release July 5 - will prove strong and boost the\par yen back up.\par    "There are still worries that the tankan will lift the\par yen quite a bit," said Yasushi Muramatsu, who overseas\par fixed-income trading at Daiwa Securities America Inc. in New\par York. "Right now, the mood is still very cautious."\par    An Opportunity To Bail Out?\par    Traders also cite worries - shared by local and overseas\par players alike - that the Federal Reserve may tighten its\par monetary policy further after the widely-expected\par one-quarter percentage point interest-rate increase at its\par policy-setting meeting on June 29 and 30.\par    "(Japanese) short-term players are only worried about\par where the yields will be at the end of the quarter, whereas\par longer-term investors are more concerned with the Fed than\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," the sales manager said.\par    Treasurys staged a modest advance overnight Monday, when\par Japanese life insurers and trust banks were said to have\par bought five-year and 10-year maturities to take advantage of\par the dollar's climb following the latest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par    But a sharp sell-off during the U.S. trading - led by\par hedge funds, mortgage-related accounts and underwriters of\par corporate and agency deals - more than offset those gains.\par    "An incident like this will make (Japanese investors)\par even more reluctant to buy Treasurys," Muramatsu said.\par    Furthermore, recent speculation that the Japanese\par government is supporting the euro in an effort to allow its\par investors to bail out of loss-making euro-denominated assets\par may also hold true with their U.S. investments, said another\par New York-based sales manager.\par    "There are a lot of players who bought (U.S. assets)\par over the years at Y125 and even Y140," said the sales\par manager. "As far as I'm concerned, this is the government's\par way of telling investors to bail out while the yen's\par relatively weak," and their unrealized losses on U.S.\par investments relatively small, he added.\par    Those investors were hit by both the yen's appreciation\par and the falling yields on Treasurys over the past decades,\par he noted.\par    The euro's prolonged decline against the yen this year\par fueled rumors that Japanese life insurers were selling off\par euro zone bonds to cut losses.\par    The BOJ's euro-buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Friday was widely\par believed to have been an attempt to rescue those investors.\par Before the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , some major life insurance firms\par denied that they were making major change in their global\par fixed-income portfolio.\par    -Sonoko Setaishi; 201-938-4148;\par sonoko.setaishi@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  06-21-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1713EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010826dv6l013es\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
