\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Prodi hits euro, BOJ hits yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 1,163 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 1999\par 17:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 21 (Reuters) - Euro-negative comments from European Commission President-designate Romano Prodi unravelled much of the Bank of Japan's work in weakening the yen on Monday, making the dollar the winner in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Prodi said Italy might find it hard to stay in the euro if the country kept losing competitiveness longer term, hurting euro/dollar and knocking euro/yen from three-week highs set overnight after yen sales by Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Prodi, a former prime minister of Italy, later said his comments had been reported in an "ambiguous and wrong way". \par \par \uc2  But the euro kept on falling, to within three-quarters of a cent of its life lows against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Prodi was foolish to say anything along those lines, given the leeway for interpretation," said Michael Metcalfe, treasury analyst at NatWest Global Financial Markets. \par \par \uc2  "Talk on its own is not going to be enough to keep the euro strong - we need stronger economic data." \par \par \uc2  The euro was trading at $1.0315/20 at 1456 GMT, from lows of $1.0309, overnight highs of $1.0420 and from $1.0368/78 in late European trade on Friday. The euro reached a life low of $1.0254 on June 7. \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen fell by more than a yen from its highs, to 126.00/10, but remained well above lows of 125.24 reached before the Bank of Japan sold yen for dollars in Asia, its fourth round of yen-selling in two weeks. \par \par \uc2  Weakening exports appeared to be worrying the Japanese authorities after Japan's May customs-cleared trade surplus came in overnight at 31.5 percent below year earlier figures. \par \par \uc2  Teis Knuthsen, head of strategy at SEB Merchant Banking in Stockholm, said the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's primary concern was euro/yen, even though it had frequently bought dollars, rather than euros, for yen. \par \par \uc2  "Every time the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect dollar/yen levels, we have been at around 124 in euro/yen," said Knuthsen. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese investors were buying into the euro heavily at the beginning of the year and a lot of them were unhedged - they have had losses in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and on their bond positions." \par \par \uc2  Senior Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Eisuke Sakakibara said on Monday that previous BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday to curb yen strength was not aimed at specific investors. \par \par \uc2  He also said that authorities in Europe, Japan and the U.S. were monitoring \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, and could cooperate. \par \par \uc2  Traders are becoming more convinced about Japan's determination to prevent yen strength from hurting its emerging economic recovery and are wary of betting against the dollar for now, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 122.19/24 yen from overnight three-week highs of 122.55, more than 1-1/2 yen above lows of 120.60. \par \par \uc2  The euro looked more vulnerable, in contrast, analysts said, even after Prodi clarified his earlier comments and said there was no risk of Italy leaving the euro because of the small inflation gap between Italy and its partners. \par \par \uc2  Supportive comments from Bundesbank officials Ernst Welteke and Hans Tietmeyer also failed to help Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "A lot of people are very sceptical that we will get a Euroland recovery in the second half," said Knuthsen. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, oil-rich Norway's crown reached record highs against the euro for the fourth consecutive trading day, despite last week's cut in official interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Norway's deposit rate at six percent remained attractive compared with the U.S. or the euro zone, and rate cuts were likely to have a stimulative effect on the Norwegian economy, so boosting the crown. \par \par \uc2  But the euro kept on falling, to within three-quarters of a cent of its life lows against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Prodi was foolish to say anything along those lines, given the leeway for interpretation," said Michael Metcalfe, treasury analyst at NatWest Global Financial Markets. \par \par \uc2  "Talk on its own is not going to be enough to keep the euro strong - we need stronger economic data." \par \par \uc2  The euro was trading at $1.0315/20 at 1456 GMT, from lows of $1.0309, overnight highs of $1.0420 and from $1.0368/78 in late European trade on Friday. The euro reached a life low of $1.0254 on June 7. \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen fell by more than a yen from its highs, to 126.00/10, but remained well above lows of 125.24 reached before the Bank of Japan sold yen for dollars in Asia, its fourth round of yen-selling in two weeks. \par \par \uc2  Weakening exports appeared to be worrying the Japanese authorities after Japan's May customs-cleared trade surplus came in overnight at 31.5 percent below year earlier figures. \par \par \uc2  Teis Knuthsen, head of strategy at SEB Merchant Banking in Stockholm, said the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's primary concern was euro/yen, even though it had frequently bought dollars, rather than euros, for yen. \par \par \uc2  "Every time the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect dollar/yen levels, we have been at around 124 in euro/yen," said Knuthsen. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese investors were buying into the euro heavily at the beginning of the year and a lot of them were unhedged - they have had losses in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and on their bond positions." \par \par \uc2  Senior Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Eisuke Sakakibara said on Monday that previous BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday to curb yen strength was not aimed at specific investors. \par \par \uc2  He also said that authorities in Europe, Japan and the U.S. were monitoring \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, and could cooperate. \par \par \uc2  Traders are becoming more convinced about Japan's determination to prevent yen strength from hurting its emerging economic recovery and are wary of betting against the dollar for now, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 122.19/24 yen from overnight three-week highs of 122.55, more than 1-1/2 yen above lows of 120.60. \par \par \uc2  The euro looked more vulnerable, in contrast, analysts said, even after Prodi clarified his earlier comments and said there was no risk of Italy leaving the euro because of the small inflation gap between Italy and its partners. \par \par \uc2  Supportive comments from Bundesbank officials Ernst Welteke and Hans Tietmeyer also failed to help Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "A lot of people are very sceptical that we will get a Euroland recovery in the second half," said Knuthsen. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, oil-rich Norway's crown reached record highs against the euro for the fourth consecutive trading day, despite last week's cut in official interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Norway's deposit rate at six percent remained attractive compared with the U.S. or the euro zone, and rate cuts were likely to have a