\par\par \b \uc2  BOND MARKETS - Slide in U.S.-driven selloff. \b0\par\par\uc2 626 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 1999\par 18:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 21 (Reuters) - Major government bonds market fell sharply on Monday, led by a selloff in U.S. Treasuries after prices fell through key technical support levels, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "There's no specific news driving the move. I think basically after the rally of last week a lot of the investors are starting to get a little bit nervous about Treasuries," said Danyelle Guyatt, fixed-income strategist with Deutsche Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasuries were stronger overnight in Asian and European trading, helped by gains in the dollar after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  started the week by aggressively buying dollars and later euros, bankers said. The latest move comes on the heels of a Friday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in which the BOJ unexpectedly bought euro for yen through the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But Treasuries turned weaker shortly after the North American session began, with reports of hedge fund selling and corporate supply combining to weigh on prices. \par \par \uc2  Treasuries in turn weighed on euro zone government debt which was already feeling the sting of comments by a key European Union official. \par \par \uc2  European Commission-designate Romano Prodi was reported to have said Italy risked losing its place in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if its costs remained higher than in other euro nations. \par \par \uc2  He later said these comments were taken out of context but the damage was done and the euro debt could not stage a comeback. \par \par \uc2  "The market is really bearing down on the fact that the new president of the European commission is saying this, but more importantly a man who knows the finances, aspects and problems that Italy will face in the next couple of years," said Olivier Bonnefoy, a fund broker with Fimat Banque SA in London. \par \par \uc2  Italian government bonds, or BTPs, underperformed on the news with the 10-year spread to Bund yields widening to as much as 33 basis points from 27 basis points at the open. \par \par \uc2  "Europe was in poor shape but got driven lower by the selloff in the States," said John Ratcliffe, senior money markets analyst with Thomson Global Markets. \par \par \uc2  In Britain, gilt prices were hurt by both the weakness in Treasuries and the comments from Prodi. Analysts also expressed concern about Britain's policy towards the euro. \par \par \uc2  British opposition politicians on Monday accused British Prime Minister Tony Blair of backing away from joining Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the face of strong public opposition. \par \par \uc2  Blair used his coolest language to date on the euro in weekend interviews from the G8 summit of industrial powers in Cologne, saying it would be "daft" to go in straight away. \par \par \uc2  The government insisted that Blair had not changed his position of preparing to join subject to certain conditions and winning a referendum but British newspapers and opposition politicians suggest there was backsliding. \par \par \uc2   This is how major world bond markets looked at 1728 GMT on \par \par \uc2  Monday \par \par \uc2      UNITED STATES - \par \par \uc2  Futures: September T-bonds 114-28/32, down   28/32 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab    September Eurodollars 94.580, unchanged\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Cash: Long bond at  89-03/32, down   28/32, yield 6.045 pct\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     EURO -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Futures: September Bunds on Eurex 111.33, down 0.91\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab    September Euribor 97.295, down 0.025\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Cash:    10-year Bund 97.40, up 0.81 yield 4.319 pct\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BRITAIN -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Futures: September Long gilts on LIFFE 111.91, down 0.93\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab    September short sterling 94.930, down 0.010\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Cash:    10-year gilt 105.99, down 0.84 yield 5.007 pct\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     JAPAN -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Futures: June Euroyen in Tokyo  down 99.890\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     ((Jeffrey Hodgson, London Capital Markets, +44 171 542\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 8663, fax +44 171 542 5285, jeffrey.jh.hodgson@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6l063sh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
