\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Sakakibara OKs yen drop as Japan exports sink. \b0\par\par\uc2  By William Mallard \par \uc2 702 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 1999\par 12:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 21 (Reuters) - Japan sold its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  again on Monday and signalled it was willing to let the yen drop sharply in order to support the economy and help the nation's flagging exports. \par \par \uc2  The aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) and strong words for a weaker yen from top financial diplomat Eisuke Sakakibara came just as fresh data showed exports in May slumped across the board. \par \par \uc2  "Further weakening of the yen is possible if it is necessary for the economy," said Sakakibara, Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs. "We are flexible on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Tokyo authorities are ready to "take decisive action" again to stem a premature yen rise, he told reporters. "What is of paramount importance is to ensure Japan's economic recovery as soon as possible." \par \par \uc2  On the heels of a Friday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in which the BOJ unexpectedly bought euro for yen through the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the BOJ started the week by aggressively buying dollars and, in late afternoon, euros, Tokyo bankers said. The moves follow two other recent dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The BOJ was spotted in the market early in the morning around 120.70 yen and spent an estimated $5 billion in aggressive yen-selling bursts to drive the dollar as high as 122.55 yen. It was around 122.30 yen at 7 p.m. (1000 GMT). \par \par \uc2  Tokyo shares rallied strongly as exporters' stocks surged, with the benchmark Nikkei stock index climbing 307.59 points or almost 1.8 percent to 17,738.85, its highest close since October 1997. \par \par \uc2  "We're now beginning to see a recognition that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to be used to support economic growth," said economist Andrew Shipley at Schroder Securities Japan. \par \par \uc2  "This shift in policy is significant. It could point to substantial yen weakness in the days ahead as the government finally resorts to using a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up a continued fragile economic recovery." \par \par \uc2  A strong yen hurts the Japanese economy by making its exports more expensive in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and by shrinking the yen value of Japanese companies' overseas earnings. \par \par \uc2  A weaker yen also helps fend off deflation, which remains a threat despite half a year of fiscal and monetary stimulus and huge cash infusions into the banking sector, Shipley said. \par \par \uc2  Although many in the market questioned the staying power of the BOJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the aggressive yen-selling challenged the market view that the authorities simply wanted to defend the dollar at 120 yen. \par \par \uc2  Some also believe the Japanese authorities have abandoned a presumed stance of trying to keep the dollar below 125 yen. \par \par \uc2  One of Sakakibara's predecessors at the Finance Ministry, Kosuke Nakahira, said the authorities were sending a clear signal that the market's bullishness on the yen was an overreaction to the unexpectedly strong 1.9 percent rise in economic growth in the January-March period from the previous quarter. \par \par \uc2  "I think (the BOJ and the Finance Ministry) are very much afraid the market's overreaction to this could nip the recovery in the bud," Nakahira said. \par \par \uc2  Underscoring the economy's weakness, an industry association reported on Monday that orders received by Japanese machine tool makers dropped 34 percent in May from a year earlier, the 15th monthly decline in a row. \par \par \uc2  Overnight in Cologne, Germany, Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi reiterated to his Group of Eight partners his pledge to break Japan's unprecedented two-year economic slide and eke out growth of 0.5 percent in the fiscal year to next March. \par \par \uc2  Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came as the government reported \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exports dropped 11.8 percent in May from a year earlier, outpacing a 3.3 percent import decline to narrow the nation's trade surplus for the second consecutive month. \par \par \uc2  Chief economist Chris Calderwood at Jardine Fleming Securities (Asia) in Tokyo said exports sank on a delayed reaction to the yen's rise from last October and because Japan's export markets are not as robust as many had thought. ((Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432-8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6l062s8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
