\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Yen soft after aggressive BOJ action. \b0\par\par\uc2 542 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 1999\par 11:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 21 (Reuters) - The yen was soft, holding near three-week lows against the dollar and the euro in European trading on Monday as repeated Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the market extremely nervous. In addition to BOJ yen selling, the Finance Ministry's Eisuke "Mr. Yen" Sakakibara said Japan did not intend to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in narrow bands - which some traders took as a signal Japan would tolerate a sharply lower yen. \par \par \uc2  "That's certainly the impression that the authorities are trying to give," said Francis Breedon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Lehman Brothers in London. \par \par \uc2  The yen fell back in early European trading after dealers in Tokyo said the Bank of Japan channelled yen sales for euros through the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, as it did on Friday. \par \par \uc2  But traders in London who had dealt with European central banks on Friday played down such talk. The ECB declined to comment on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation emanating from Asia. \par \par \uc2  Traders were unsure how lasting an impact the BOJ's yen-selling - the fourth round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in less than two weeks - would have, given the choppy actions that followed earlier bouts of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The question is whether the market wants to take it higher," Breedon at Lehman Brothers said. \par \par \uc2  "At what point does the market give up testing whether they can push it through 120 (yen)?" \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 122.22/27 yen at 0842 GMT from the 122.55 high hit overnight and 120.44/49 late in Europe on Friday. It was seen struggling to top 122.50 given heavy hedge-selling interest from Japanese investors and exporters. \par \par \uc2  The euro also rose to a three-week high of 127.35 yen, up 2-1/2 yen from 124.84/03 late on Friday and up nearly four percent from Friday's life low of 122.53. \par \par \uc2  Traders said there was some confusion about whether the Japanese authorities were going to tolerate a higher dollar or whether they were trying to quash expectations that they wanted the dollar to stay in recent ranges near 120 yen. \par \par \uc2  "Judging from what Mr. Sakakibara himself was saying when we were near 125 yen, it's difficult to believe they want it much beyond that," said a dealer at a Japanese bank in London. "It may be that they just want the spotlight off 120." \par \par \uc2  The euro drifted off early highs but held near the $1.04 level, as the market consolidated after Friday's rally spurred by the BOJ's euro/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the weekend. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0389/94 at 0852 GMT versus $1.0368/78 late in European session on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the euro appeared to have built a short-term base for now and could stay range-bound ahead of the U.S. Federal Open Market Committee meeting scheduled for June 29-30. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was stable at $1.5937/47, virtually unchanged from late Friday's $1.5933/38. \par \par \uc2  Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was a touch softer, trading at 1.5382/92 against 1.5411/20 late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  ((Shin Kishima, London Capital Markets +44-171-542-6721)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6l062c5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
