\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW-BOJ eyes stable  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2 , not specific rate. \b0\par\par\uc2 282 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 1999\par 06:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 21 (Reuters) - Japanese monetary authorities sent a clear message by selling yen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Monday that they want exchange rate stability, former top Japanese financial diplomat Kosuke Nakahira said. \par \par \uc2  Nakahira, former vice finance minister for international affairs, told Reuters Television the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) and Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) were seeking with the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to block drastic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, not to achieve a specific dollar/yen rate. \par \par \uc2  "The authorities are sending a very clear message to the market," said Nakahira, vice chairman of the Intstitute for International Economic Studies. \par \par \uc2  The markets had pushed up the yen in an overreaction to Japan's unexpectedly strong January-March gross domestic product data released on June 10, he said. \par \par \uc2  "Even if we acknowledge that first quarter GDP was good, the economic recovery has just begun," Nakahira said. "I think (the BOJ and MOF) are very much afraid the market's overreaction to this could nip the recovery in the bud." \par \par \uc2  At this fragile stage, when the government's top priority is ensuring a recovery, sharp movements in the yen or long-term interest rates are the auhtorities' biggest worry, Nakahira said. \par \par \uc2  "They want to create a stable market and avoid a situation in which they don't know at all the prospects for how far the markets will go," he said. \par \par \uc2  He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also partly pre-emptive, as the authorities were concerned that market movements could accelerate since the caution that preceded the weekend Group of Eight summit had vanished. ((Miho Kamizaki, Tokyo newsroom +81-3 3432-8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6l062ai\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
