\par\par \b \uc2  US Treasuries benefit from dlr's hefty gain vs yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 506 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 1999\par 07:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 21 (Reuters) - Most U.S. Treasuries gained moderately by midsession on Monday, benefiting from the dollar's hefty rise against the yen as a result of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (BOJ) aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars for yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's rise of nearly two yen this morning is the only reason (behind the rise in Treasuries) I can think of," said a dealer at a brokerage. \par \par \uc2  But overall trade was thin as yen-based investors were seen to have been sidelined while watching to see how much more the dollar could gain against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also said investors backed off from creating new positions actively before a Federal Open Market Committee meeting scheduled for June 29-30, and also due to a lack of crucial economic data until Thursday. \par \par \uc2  At 0357 GMT, the benchmark 30-year bond was at 90-12/32, yielding 5.94 percent, as compared to 89-31/32 to yield 5.98 percent in late New York on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The 5.25 percent two-year note was at 99-15/32, yielding 5.54 percent, up slightly from 99-14/32 in New York on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Trade has been quiet with scattered bids in long-end futures contracts. Other than that, there hasn't been much going on," said the dealer. \par \par \uc2  The dollar shot up almost two yen to reach a three-week high against the yen on early Monday as the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively to suppress the yen. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars in waves from 120.70 yen to a high of 122.55, spending a rumoured $5.0 billion or more in the process. \par \par \uc2  Almost concurrently with BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, Japanese Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara said a premature strengthening of the yen was undesirable, but added that the ministry had no intention of containing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements within a narrow range. \par \par \uc2  "I have three things to tell you. One, we do not want a premature strengthening of the yen. Two, we do not intend to contain \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates within a narrow range. Three, we do not plan any target zones," Sakakibara told reporters inside the ministry. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Treasuries were sensitive to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and asset market movements now as there was no crucial economic data in sight until Thursday with the release of May durable goods orders, and then the FOMC meeting on June 29-30. \par \par \uc2  Federal Reserve policy-makers are widely expected to raise the federal funds rate by 25 basis points to 5.0 percent, which is seen as a pre-emptive inflation fighter aiding the bond market. \par \par \uc2  Another trader said that sentiment was still steady. \par \par \uc2  The September T-bond futures contract stood at 116-1/32, up 9/32 from the previous close. It was moving in a range of 115-24/32 to 116-2/32. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 5473-3748 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6l061rk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
