\par\par \b \uc2  JGBs mixed in midday Tokyo, boosted by  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  moves. \b0\par\par\uc2 596 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 1999\par 05:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 21 (Reuters) - Japanese government bond (JGB) prices were mixed by midday Tokyo on Monday as futures got a slight boost from Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) dollar-buying, yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite the cash market's overall weakness. \par \par \uc2  A pessimistic outlook for the 10-year JGB auction scheduled for Tuesday pressured the 10-year sector, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the key 213th 10-year cash bond rose briefly to 1.755 percent in morning trade. It was last quoted at 1.740 percent, compared with Friday's close of 1.730 percent. \par \par \uc2  The key September JGB futures contract closed the morning session up 0.15 at 131.50. \par \par \uc2  The September contract was lifted on the back of the BOJ's dollar-buying action, as it reinforced views that the government was not confident about the economy. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, the contract pared early losses on short-covering after the summit of the Group of Seven produced few fresh incentives for the market. \par \par \uc2  The G7 pledged to work closely together towards the goal of solid economic growth, and President Bill Clinton urged Japanese Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi on Friday to use "all available tools" to spur growth. \par \par \uc2  But the G7 leaders roundly praised Japan's efforts to boost the economy and Obuchi repeated his determination to achieve positive growth for the current fiscal year. \par \par \uc2  "The summit leaders highly regarded Japan's efforts. And strong first-quarter growth should allow the government to reach its positive growth target, so the summit had no impact on the market," a trader at a bank-affiliated brokerage said. \par \par \uc2  But traders were wary of chasing prices in either direction before the auction. Turnover was a meagre 11,993 lots for the morning session. \par \par \uc2  Traders expect the September contract's gains to be limited near 132 yen. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, traders said some brokerages were selling the 10-year sector to make room for 10-year bonds to be auctioned on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) will offer 1.4 trillion yen worth of 10-year JGBs on Tuesday. Traders expect the coupon to be set at 1.7 or 1.8 percent. \par \par \uc2  Speculation over the economy, monetary policy, forthcoming supply and technical impediments will keep a negative bias on the auction, traders said. \par \par \uc2  But the market may be supported by bargain-hunting in the secondary market and by investor demand for corporate issuances. \par \par \uc2  "We don't see a whole lot of investor demand at the auction. But if prices fall, investors will likely pick up the bonds in the secondary market," the brokerage trader said. \par \par \uc2  Another trader at a city bank said: "There may be some demand because of a lot of redemptions in June." \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the key March TIBOR-based euroyen futures contract was little changed at 99.63, versus Friday's day-session close of 99.65. \par \par \uc2  Three-month certificates of deposit (CD) starting in July, which would cover settlements in September - the close of the first half of the current fiscal year ending in March - were issued at 0.04 percent, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Other three-month CDs were offered at 0.03 percent, but remained untraded. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ maintained a net surplus of 1.0 trillion yen in the money market after its regular 0020 GMT \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , unchanged from levels seen after Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The key overnight call rate was traded at 0.03 percent, unchanged from Friday's most heavily traded rate. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8027 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6l05vy9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
