\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Sakakibara Calls ECB  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  'Very Efficient' \b0\par\par\uc2 451 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 1999\par 14:50\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Friday by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of Japan to support the euro versus the yen was "very efficient," a top official at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Monday. \par \par \uc2  In an interview with a small group of reporters, Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara also warned that the U.S. and Europe stand ready to join Japan as needed in order to correct \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels when they get out of line. \par \par \uc2  "A tripartite system for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy cooperation is in place. If needed, we will cooperate." \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said he is keeping a close eye on the yen's moves against the dollar as well as the euro. \par \par \uc2  "The time when we only think about the dollar/yen has passed. Since I think we have a tripartite system in place, I want to closely watch moves in both the dollar/yen and the euro/yen," Sakakibara said. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara reiterated that an appreciation of the yen before Japan's economy recovers is unwelcome and that Japanese monetary officials stand ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as needed. \par \par \uc2  "At this stage, a premature strengthening of the yen is undesirable. We have an extremely strong will to stop this. If needed, we are ready to take decisive action," Sakakibara said. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is aimed at bringing about a recovery in the economy at the earliest date and isn't made with specific target zones or ranges for the yen in mind. \par \par \uc2  "For us, the most important thing is ensuring a recovery in the economy as fast as possible," Sakakibara said. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is decided with this in mind. \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy isn't formulated with a specific range in mind." \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said that while preventing a premature yen rise is the most important thing at this point, what is most important to ensure an economic recovery can change from situation to situation. \par \par \uc2  "Naturally, if there is a situation where a strengthening of the yen is necessary, a strengthening of the yen again is possible. But is if a weakening of the yen is necessary, a weakening of the yen is naturally possible. We are flexible on that point." \par \par \uc2  Asked about the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday, Sakakibara said, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  took "appropriate action," but said the move wasn't aimed at supporting the downside of a specific range. \par \par \uc2  Asked if the Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the euro against the yen was part of an effort to help stem losses of Japanese investors holding euro-denominated assets, Sakakibara said that the action was taken without regard for specific investors. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv6l02wq1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
