\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervenes \highlight\uc2  Again, Sending Dollar Soaring Against Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 1,016 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 1999\par 06:58\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The dollar surged against the yen Monday after the Bank of Japan, seeing a too-strong yen as a threat to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fragile economic recovery, again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the strength of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The euro, meanwhile, lost ground against the dollar after a European official raised concerns about the future of European economic and monetary union (EMU). \par \par \uc2  Late Monday in New York, the dollar was quoted at 122.49 yen, up sharply from 120.45 yen late Friday in New York. The euro was quoted at $1.0351, down from $1.0391 late Friday in New York. The British pound was quoted at $1.5918, down from $1.5963 in New York late Friday. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ is now showing yen enthusiasts it's ready to do battle, pulling out all the stops and pouring some $20 billion into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to prevent yen appreciation from foiling the country's efforts at economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  marked the fourth time in 10 days the BOJ has taken steps to curb the yen's rise. The action represents a massive - and costly - effort by the bank to counter upward pressure on the yen brought on by improving sentiment toward Japan's economy. A stronger yen makes it tougher for Japanese exporters to sell their products overseas. \par \par \uc2  Most of Monday's advance in the dollar came in Asian dealings, with little follow-through dollar buying against the yen in the New York session. \par \par \uc2  "As long as the Bank of Japan pursues an overly tight monetary policy, people are not going to be that encouraged" to go along with yen weakness, said Mike Rosenberg, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Deutsche Bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  For weeks, many \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders had been convinced that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed to keep the dollar somewhere between 120 yen and 125 yen. Those expectations were confirmed when the BOJ started buying dollars at the low 117-yen level a little more than a week ago. \par \par \uc2  But with Monday's action, it appears the Finance Ministry and the BOJ are even more worried than the market had thought about the possibility of a further strengthening in the yen. \par \par \uc2  In a rare move, Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara called a small group of reporters to his office at the end of the Asian trading day to confirm that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had taken place and to warn that more could come at any time. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara, whose knack for influencing exchange rates has earned him the moniker "Mr. Yen," timed his comments for the start of the European trading day, hinting at the possibility of coordinated action. "A tripartite system for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy cooperation is in place. If needed, we will cooperate." On Friday, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros for yen on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The time when we only think about the dollar-versus-yen has passed. Since I think we have a tripartite system in place, I want to closely watch moves in both the dollar-yen and the euro-yen," Sakakibara added. \par \par \uc2  Helping to improve sentiment about Japan's recovery was a report earlier this month that the country's gross domestic product surged 1.9% in the first quarter. The expansion in Japan's economy was the first in 18 months, and indicated that the government's economic-stimulus measures have had some effect. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the euro weakened against the dollar after European Commission president-designate Romano Prodi reportedly said it would be difficult for Italy to remain part of the euro if Italian inflation remains at current levels. \par \par \uc2  Prodi later clarified his remarks, reportedly saying that Italy's inflation poses no risk to its euro membership in the near term. But the damage was done. \par \par \uc2  "In the context of his entire speech, his comments weren't that inflammatory," said Kathy Jones, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Prudential Securities in New York. "But it raised the specter of a country leaving monetary union. It raised the possibility that it could happen, and that's something that was supposed to be impossible." \par \par \uc2  Also, Deutsche Bundesbank official Ernst Welteke said that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against market sentiment is "useless." Investors interpreted the remarks as a warning that the ECB wouldn't bail out the euro. \par \par \uc2  Bundesbank President Hans Tietmeyer said that Germany is in part to blame for the weaker euro. "In addition to the interest-rate differential with the U.S. and the political events in Kosovo, the development of the euro's external value is also in part due to the not very impressive situation in Germany as the dominant economy" in the euro zone, Tietmeyer said in a speech at the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Hanover, Germany. \par \par \uc2  This marked the first time that Tietmeyer has publicly blamed the euro's weakness on Germany in particular. \par \par \uc2  In other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealings, the dollar was quoted at 1.5415 Swiss francs, up from 1.5378 Swiss francs late Friday in New York. It also was quoted at 1.4710 Canadian dollars, up from C$1.4641 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  In Mexico City, the peso ended flat against the dollar. The peso was quoted at 9.3800 per dollar, the same as Friday's close in Mexico City. \par \par \uc2  The Brazilian real ended trading at 1.7590 to the dollar, unchanged from Friday's close. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a bit of a downside, however, as market participants await a reduction in the country's benchmark lending-rate target expected Wednesday, from its current 22%, one trader in New York said. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's Finance Minister Pedro Malan said Monday if the country continues to remain on track fiscally, real interest rates could drop to near 10% or lower by year-end. \par \par \uc2  "If we continue to do the right things on the fiscal front, (such a rate drop) is within our grasp," Malan said at a press briefing at the Council of the Americas. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv6l02wev\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
