\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Remains Pressured To Achieve Sustainable Growth \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikako Mogi \par \uc2 882 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 1999\par 18:41\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  COLOGNE (Dow Jones)--While leaders of the world's seven richest nations extended some praise to Japanese efforts to revitalize its economy, Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi remains under scrutiny to prove that the government measures lead to a self-sustaining recovery. \par \par \uc2  Just as in the past year, leaders of the Group of Seven again called on Japan to do more to ensure it is on a domestic-led growth path. But the tone of the reference in the economic communique was much friendlier than in the previous one, where specific requests were included. \par \par \uc2  Among "encouraging developments" in global economies, the communique cited positive actions by Japan. It then went on to say that "in Japan, it is still essential to implement stimulus measures until domestic demand led growth is restored and to pursue structural measures to enhance the economy's efficiency and competitiveness." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said as the economic communique was within their expectations, effects on financial markets will be limited. "I think the summit will reaffirm the dollar's downside versus the yen but won't trigger any sharp moves in either direction deviating from the recent levels around Y120," said Ryutaro Kono, senior economist at Dai-Ichi Life Research Institute in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Obuchi reiterated his "resolute determination" to attain the government's goal of a 0.5% economic growth for the current fiscal year, which ends next March. \par \par \uc2  At a news conference after the Group of Eight leading nations' summit, Obuchi said that the Japanese government will take every measure available to ensure stable growth. \par \par \uc2  "I told my counterparties that I am tackling the task with a resolute determination to ensure that recent positive signs of economic growth become more solid and to take root and achieve a 0.5% growth," Obuchi said. \par \par \uc2  In his meetings with G-7 counterparties, Obuchi emphasized measures the Japanese government has taken thus far and reiterated his resolve to do his utmost and continue efforts to keep the economy going forward. \par \par \uc2  At the same time, given reservations shown by other leaders on the recent positive economic data - a strong annualized 7.9% rate of gross domestic growth in the first quarter - Obuchi refrained from expressing outright optimism over the future course of the economy. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I feel very fortunate to be attending the summit meeting (after these\par developments,)" he said, also referring to a decision on the day he left\par for the summit Thursday to extend the current parliamentary session\par until Aug. 13 to deliver bills related to his job creation and\par industrial competitiveness measures. Noting that it would likely be\par difficult for Japan to achieve such a high growth rate in the the second\par quarter, Obuchi said "I'm not complacent with the (first quarter\par figures.)"\par    Extra Budget Seen Inevitable Later This Year\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Analysts generally see the need for an extra budget focusing on public\par works spending later in the year when effects from past pump-priming\par measures fade. They say the stronger-than-expected first quarter GDP\par figures reflected a rebound from a very weak showing in the previous\par quarter as well as front-loading of some positive sentiment in\par anticipation for more government stimulus steps this year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Economic indicators following the June 10 release of the GDP still\par failed to show a clear reversal for an economic uptrend. And\par intensifying corporate restructuring will likely push up the nation's\par unemployment rate even higher than the current record high 4.8%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It's dubious to what extend the first quarter GDP reflects the real\par economic strength," said Tamotsu Yasuda, economist at Tokyo-Mitsubishi\par Securities Co. in Tokyo. "We will probably need about Y10 trillion total\par in an extra budget to bridge the gap in falling public works orders\par later in the year."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It's too early for Obuchi to be complacent with rising public polls\par in support of his government," Yasuda said. A recent public opinion poll\par showed Obuchi's approval rating exceeding disapproval for the first time\par since he took office last September.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Just how the Japanese government feels nervous about the current state\par of the economy was underlined by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Friday\par to stem yen's appreciation versus the euro and the yen. This was the\par third time \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market in just over one\par week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Stability in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market is desirable especially at a\par time when Japan's economy is at an important juncture, said Haruhiko\par Kuroda, the head of the Finance Ministry's international bureau, on the\par sidelines of the meeting of the G-7 meetings. "We have taken appropriate\par measures in the past and will do so in the future," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bought euros for yen on behalf of the BOJ as\par the both the euro and dollar weakened against the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On non-economic issues, Japan brought to attention the developments in\par North Korea over its planned launch of ballistic missiles. pushed for an\par early entry of China into the World Trade Organization, pledged\par financial assistance of $200 million to help Russia dismantle nuclear\par submarines, and announced a $200 million financial assistance for the\par reconstruction of Kosovo and surrounding neighbors.\par -By Chikako Mogi, cmogi@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 20-06-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1641GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv6k04muc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
