\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ Concern For Japan Investors Seen In Euro Buys \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrew Joncus \par \uc2 398 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 1999\par 16:32\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  LONDON (Dow Jones)--The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buying euro for yen through the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , suggests the Bank of Japan may be more directly concerned about the plight of Japanese investors than previously thought. \par \par \uc2  "The impression had been that the Japanese authorities were concerned to prevent yen strength generally," said Paul Meggyesi, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Deutsche Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "But the fact that they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in euro/yen could be an indication that the real motivation is to enable Japanese investors to exit loss-making positions in the euro," he said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese investors' overhang of long positions in euro-denominated bonds, which official data suggest were being added to at least as late as April, have been a major factor behind the euro's 10-yen tumble over the past four weeks. \par \par \uc2  Partly causing the fall, and partly in reaction to it, Japanese investors have had to sell euro for yen to hedge positions put on at much higher euro levels. This has been happening at the same time as foreigners have been buying yen to invest in the booming Japanese stock market, further worsening the losses for those caught long of euro. \par \par \uc2  Of course, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may simply have ordered \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in euros because the euro threatened to enter free-fall, whereas the dollar had remained quite steady against the yen, trading around Y119.00 since early in Europe Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/yen was actually quite strong compared with its general level of recent months," said Jesper Dannesboe, treasury economist at ABN-Amro. "It was hard to see why the Bank of Japan would want to buy at that level." \par \par \uc2  The euro, however, had lost three yen since Wednesday to hit a new all-time low of Y122.63 just half an hour before the ECB's yen-buying on behalf of the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  At 1345 GMT, the euro was Y125.10, below its high of Y125.50 in the immediate wake of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but 2 1/2 yen off its lows just before the ECB came in. The dollar was Y120.34, down from Y120.45 just after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but more than a yen higher than it had been just before it. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 18-06-99 \par \par \uc2  1432GMT \par \par \uc2  1074 1195 4164 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv6i04l36\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
