\par\par \b \uc2  Japan confirms it sold yen to protect economy. \b0\par\par\uc2 314 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 1999\par 20:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  COLOGNE, June 18 (Reuters) - Japan sold yen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Friday to prevent large \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements from harming its fragile economic recovery, said Haruhiko Kuroda, director-general of the International Bureau of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It is a fact that we acted in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market today," Kuroda told reporters at a briefing during the World Economic Summit in Cologne. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda declined to comment on how Japan had placed the yen selling orders to the market, but the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it had sold yen for euros on behalf of the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  "Big fluctuations in (the Japanese) \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  are unfavourable, particularly at a time when the economy is at quite a sensitive stage and has yet to embark on a full-fledged recovery," Kuroda said. \par \par \uc2  Japan has said stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements are favourable, but, in reality, are not easily achievable, Kuroda said. \par \par \uc2  Asked why \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  chose the euro instead of the dollar as an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tool, Kuroda said: "We took this decision bearing in mind recent market developments." He did elaborate. \par \par \uc2  The ECB's surprise yen selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the euro on behalf of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  boosted the value of the euro as well as dollar against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said Japan wanted not only to influence the value of the yen, but also to boost the euro portfolio held by Japan's official \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve. \par \par \uc2  Japan had the world's largest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, at $223.66 billion at the end of May. Euro members had \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves totalling $280.5 billion at the end of March. \par \par \uc2  The majority of Japan's reserves is believed to be denominated in dollars, but Tokyo has expressed a willingness to hold euros also as a reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6i061v1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
