\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Yen pounded after  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 1,192 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 1999\par 18:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 18 (Reuters) - The yen slumped against the euro on Friday, losing over three yen as the European Central Banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling yen for euros on behalf of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Analysts said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was triggered by a fall to lifetime lows in euro/yen. The yen had also been firm against the dollar despite a warning from Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Eisuke Sakakibara overnight that Japan would act on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets when necessary. \par \par \uc2  "The alltime low on euro/yen was psychologically becoming a bit of a concern for the Bank of Japan," said David Brickman, international economist at PaineWebber. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/yen was falling but not dramatically and because of the weakness of the euro the situation focused on euro/yen." \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen was at 125.09/22 yen by 1524 GMT, compared with a high of 125.86 reached after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and with a lifetime low set earlier on Friday at 122.60. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was at 120.56/61, after bouncing from the day's low at 119.00 and hitting a high at 120.94. The dollar was at 119.67/72 yen in late European trade on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "It seems the bottom is around 118 in dollar/yen and 123 in euro/yen and the Bank of Japan will not allow the yen to go below," said Michael Rottmann, strategist at HypoVereinsbank. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the Bank of Japan was concerned that too fast an appreciation of the yen would set back Japan's still fragile economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "The authorities are worried that the first quarter GDP figures (released last week) don't accurately represent what is going on realistically in the private sector of the economy and on the net export side," said Brickman at PaineWebber. \par \par \uc2  "If we were to get a strengthening in yen, one would expect that to feed through into a further deterioration of the trade surplus and be a further dampener on growth going forward." \par \par \uc2  He said an unstable yen would also slow the recovery in the rest of Asia. \par \par \uc2  "As much as a weak yen is a problem, so is a strong yen. Stability is needed for those countries and the quicker the rest of Asia recovers, it will help \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  via its export market," Brickman said. \par \par \uc2  However, he warned \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not work in the long run unless it was backed up by policy. He added that he expected the government to come up with a significant fiscal supplementary package in the coming months to stimulate domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  At a meeting in Cologne, U.S. President Bill Clinton and urged Japanese Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi to use all available tools to spur Japanese economic growth. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves in other \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets took their cue from the yen. \par \par \uc2  Euro/dollar jumped to a high of $1.0422 after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , from a low of $1.0295. The euro was last at $1.0370/72. \par \par \uc2  Sterling also rose against the dollar, to a high of $1.6001 from $1.5905. The pound has since dropped back to $1.5924/29. \par \par \uc2  Rottmann at HypoVereinsbank in Munich, said over the next few weeks Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's testimony on Thursday would continue to support the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Greenspan made clear the Fed is still ahead of curve and yesterday's testimony was a clear indication the Fed will hike rates by 25 basis points at the end of the month," he said. \par \par \uc2  "The Fed being ahead of the curve translates into ongoing solid support for equity market and that is one of main driving forces for euro/dollar." \par \par \uc2  Rottmann said euro/dollar was likely to stay in a $.102 to $1.07 in the coming weeks. \par \par \uc2  ((Deepthi Wickremasinghe, London Capital Markets, +44 171 542 4040, fax +44 171 542 5285, deepthi.wickremasinghe@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/yen was falling but not dramatically and because of the weakness of the euro the situation focused on euro/yen." \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen was at 125.09/22 yen by 1524 GMT, compared with a high of 125.86 reached after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and with a lifetime low set earlier on Friday at 122.60. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was at 120.56/61, after bouncing from the day's low at 119.00 and hitting a high at 120.94. The dollar was at 119.67/72 yen in late European trade on Thursday. "It seems the bottom is around 118 in dollar/yen and 123 in euro/yen and the Bank of Japan will not allow the yen to go below," said Michael Rottmann, strategist at HypoVereinsbank. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the Bank of Japan was concerned that too fast an appreciation of the yen would set back Japan's still fragile economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "The authorities are worried that the first quarter GDP figures (released last week) don't accurately represent what is going on realistically in the private sector of the economy and on the net export side," said Brickman at PaineWebber. \par \par \uc2  "If we were to get a strengthening in yen, one would expect that to feed through into a further deterioration of the trade surplus and be a further dampener on growth going forward." \par \par \uc2  He said an 