\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of Japan Concern For Japanese Investors Seen In Euro Buying \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrew Joncus, Staff Reporter \par \uc2 780 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 1999\par 16:47\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  LONDON -(Dow Jones)- The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buying euro for yen through the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , suggests the Bank of Japan may be more directly concerned about the plight of Japanese investors than previously thought. \par \par \uc2  "The impression had been that the Japanese authorities were concerned to prevent yen strength generally," said Paul Meggyesi, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Deutsche Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "But the fact that they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in euro/yen could be an indication that the real motivation is to enable Japanese investors to exit loss-making positions in the euro," he said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese investors' overhang of long positions in euro-denominated bonds, which official data suggest were being added to at least as late as April, have been a major factor behind the euro's 10-yen tumble over the past four weeks. \par \par \uc2  Partly causing the fall, and partly in reaction to it, Japanese investors have had to sell euro for yen to hedge positions put on at much higher euro levels. This has been happening at the same time as foreigners have been buying yen to invest in the booming Japanese stock market, further worsening the losses for those caught long of euro. \par \par \uc2  Of course, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may simply have ordered \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in euros because the euro threatened to enter free-fall, whereas the dollar had remained quite steady against the yen, trading around 119.00 yen since early in Europe Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/yen was actually quite strong compared with its general level of recent months," said Jesper Dannesboe, treasury economist at ABN-Amro. "It was hard to see why the Bank of Japan would want to buy at that level." \par \par \uc2  The euro, however, had lost three yen since Wednesday to hit a new all-time low of 122.63 yen just half an hour before the ECB's yen-buying on behalf of the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  At 9:45 a.m. EDT, the euro was 125.10 yen, below its high of 125.50 yen in the immediate wake of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but 2 1/2 yen off its lows just before the ECB came in. The dollar was at 120.34 yen, down from 120.45 yen just after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but more than a yen higher than it had been just before it. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  caught many market participants completely wrong-footed, coming at lunchtime in London, with many traders absent for the last day of the Ascot Week horseraces and with New York trading not yet properly begun. \par \par \uc2  Many dealers, seeing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  move even before they had heard the rumors that a number of central banks had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  - and not believing that coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would take place as a Group of Seven summit was just starting in Cologne - were skeptical that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had in fact taken place. Rather, they suggested, a canny market participant had used the thin market conditions to squeeze out euro short positions. \par \par \uc2  The ECB, along with the Federal Reserve Bank of New York and the Bank of Japan, initially declined to comment. \par \par \uc2  The ECB later reversed, however, with a spokesman telling reporters that "the European System of Central Banks bought euros against yen on behalf of the Bank of Japan." \par \par \uc2  While more than one ESCB bank is thought to have participated in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the ECB wouldn't comment on that or on the quantities involved. \par \par \uc2  ECB spokesman Manfred Koerber said the move was solely at the behest of the Japanese. "We charge their account with the countervalue in yen, and credit their account with euro," he said. \par \par \uc2  To some observers, that raises the question of why the ECB, some of whose members have been openly uneasy with the euro's continued slide, didn't join in with the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to give the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  more clout. \par \par \uc2  The simple answer may be that it wasn't possible to reach consensus on such a move, particularly with euro-zone decision-makers on the road, away from their central banks. \par \par \uc2  "You could also argue that they didn't have to," since the Bank of Japan was supporting the euro for them, said Meggyesi. \par \par \uc2  The euro is now at its highest level against the dollar in over two days, as a by-product of euro buying against the yen. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At 10:10 a.m. EDT it was $1.0408, down from a high of $1.0425\par immediately following the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  but more than a cent about levels\par around $1.0300 seen just before it.\par    -By Andrew Joncus; 44-171-842-9494; andrew.joncus@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1074 1195 4164 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv6i02yef\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
