\par\par \b \uc2  Sakakibara -2:Most In Mkt Already Believe No  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 159 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 1999\par 09:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Nikkei/Dow Jones)--Eisuke Sakakibara, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's vice finance minister for international affairs, declined Thursday afternoon to comment on rumors about Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Rumors circulated Tokyo banks late in the day Thursday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was checking rates or outright \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Asked about those rumors, Sakakibara said, "No comment." Sakakibara was talking to reporters at the ministry. \par \par \uc2  The rumors were sparked by a sudden jump in the dollar to around Y120.30 from Y119.90 late in the Tokyo day. Traders later said they believed dollar purchases by U.S. funds were behind the move. \par \par \uc2  A Tokyo-based trader at European bank said it appeared U.S. funds buying via U.S. banks bought a large amount of dollars in options-related trading. \par \par \uc2  At 0650 GMT, the dollar was trading around Y120.05. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 17-06-99 \par \par \uc2  0700GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv6h04kjw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
