\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Dlr bobs near 120 yen, euro eyes Ifo. \b0\par\par\uc2 560 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 1999\par 10:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 17 (Reuters) - The dollar bobbed near 120 yen in early European trading on Thursday as the market remained wary of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly below that psychologically important level. \par \par \uc2  The euro was struggling to recover from Wednesday's losses, drifting slightly above $1.03 as the market awaited the closely-watched German Ifo business survey, out at 0800 GMT. "The suspicion the market seems to have is that those (Ifo) numbers are going to be on the soft side," said Joe Prendergast, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Credit Suisse First Boston. "You can already see the euro has suffered a little." \par \par \uc2  "The feeling may be that weak numbers for the euro may be particularly positive for the yen in the very short run." \par \par \uc2  Any gains the yen makes against the euro could help bolster the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar and possibly push dollar/yen decisively below 120, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The euro stood at $1.0342/46 at 0720 GMT, slowly clawing back up after tumbling to a one-week low of $1.0285 in Wednesday's European session. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 119.98/08 yen after dipping to a low of 119.82 in late Asian trading, down from 120.22/27 late Wednesday. Euro/yen stood at 124.13/24 yen versus 123.81/94. \par \par \uc2  Roy Ruddock, a senior trader at Halewood International in London, also noted that Japanese investors had become quite jittery about their exposure to the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "A lot of Japanese names are getting worried about the euro and liquidating euro assets and taking it home," he said. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters have, on average, predicted that the Ifo climate index for western Germany would rise to 90.29 for May from 89.7 in April. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds its regular governing council meeting today, but traders did not expect a move on interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Traders expected dollar/yen to remain choppy as the dollar gravitated lower on broad selling interest but recent BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained fresh in the market's memory. \par \par \uc2  "There was rumour that the BOJ was checking rates (overnight)...and that boosted dollar/yen," a U.S. bank trader in London said. He added that the dollar subsided back to 120 yen as traders said they saw no BOJ sales of yen. \par \par \uc2  CSFB's Prendergast said that while the BOJ was seen to have expressed its displeasure about the dollar going much below 120 yen, it might wait a while before \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to avoid the impression it was protecting certain levels. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was undermined against the yen overnight as Japanese government bond yields rose and the Nikkei rallied 1.51 percent. Economic news was also generally yen-supportive. \par \par \uc2  Japan's April trade data showed imports were being boosted by a pickup in the domestic economy and Trade Minister Kaoru Yosano said Japan's April-June gross domestic product should grow from the previous quarter. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, minutes from the April 22 BOJ policy board meeting showed that the board rejected quantitative easing. \par \par \uc2  The focus is expected to turn to the testimony Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan will deliver before the Joint Economic Committee in Washington at 1400 GMT. ((Shin Kishima, London Capital Markets +44-171-542-6721)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 