\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr lower despite tame U.S. CPI, euro weak vs yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 563 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 1999\par 06:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 17 (Reuters) - The dollar was lower against the yen by midday on Thursday despite the release of tame U.S. consumer price data overnight, as a sharp rise in Tokyo stocks put the greenback on the defensive, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Selling of the euro against the yen was also pressuring the dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But dollar falls were likely to be limited as expectations were strong that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars below 120 yen, they said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 120.03/08 yen as of 0305 GMT, compared with 120.33/43 yen in late New York on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the euro stood at 123.69/85 yen as of 0317 GMT, down from 124.06 yen in late New York on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the euro was likely to test its record low of 123.18 yen, set in Tokyo on June 10, in the near term. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese investors still need to sell more euro to hedge their portfolios. Exporters are also falling behind in meeting hedging needs for euro/yen," said a dealer at a city bank. \par \par \uc2  The euro could be hit further if Germany's Ifo business climate data for May, to be released at 0800 GMT on Thursday, comes in weaker than expected, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters predicted on average that the Ifo business climate index for western Germany would rise to 90.29 for May from 89.7 in April. \par \par \uc2  Also on Thursday, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) will hold a governing council meeting. But traders said little impact was expected as the ECB would likely hold rates steady. Dealers said the dollar was likely to flutter in a narrow range against the yen ahead of U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's testimony before the Joint Economic Committee at 1400 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The market has already factored in the likelihood that the Fed will raise interest rates by 25 basis points at its Federal Open Market Committee meeting on June 29-30, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But traders will be watching to see whether Greenspan's comments offer more support of this conclusion, and also for clues as to whether he thinks a further credit-tightening beyond a 25 basis point rise might be necessary. \par \par \uc2  "I think Greenspan may say that inflationary concerns have not abated despite the CPI data, which could cause U.S. share prices to fall," said a dealer for a trust bank. \par \par \uc2  U.S. share prices and Treasuries surged overnight after data showed the overall U.S. Consumer Price Index (CPI) was flat in May after rising 0.7 percent in April. The core CPI, which excludes the volatile food and energy sector, rose 0.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  Comments by Trade Minister Kaoru Yosano, who said he thought Japan's gross domestic product for the April-June quarter would show growth from the previous quarter, helped drag down the dollar late on Thursday morning, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, Japan's Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara said that he had no comment regarding \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The euro stood at $1.0313/16 as of 0352 GMT, compared with $1.0303/08 in late New York on Wednesday. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 5473 3748 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6h06crw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
