\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Dollar appreciates Greenspan. \b0\par\par\uc2 668 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 1999\par 17:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 17 (Reuters) - The dollar came within half a cent of record highs against the euro on Thursday, following bounding U.S. asset markets after U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan indicated the need for a small rate rise. \par \par \uc2  Greenspan said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  needed to act "preemptively" to protect the U.S. economy from any "developing imbalances", suggesting a modest rise in key interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "They are going to view this rate hike as a preemptive measure, rather than the first in a long sequence," said Cameron Crise, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Warburg Dillon Read. "That should be supportive of assets, and supportive of the dollar." \par \par \uc2  U.S. stocks and bonds rose after Greenspan's testimony to U.S. lawmakers. \par \par \uc2  The euro fell to a low of $1.0290, in sight of its life low of $1.0254 set on June 7. It recovered to $1.0313/17 by 1455 GMT, from $1.0305/07 in late European trade on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Market expectations are that the U.S. Federal Open Market Committee will raise interest rates at its meeting on June 29 and 30. The federal funds rate currently stands at 4.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  The euro was also undermined after French Finance Minister Dominique Strauss-Kahn was reported in minutes of a meeting on Wednesday as saying that France's debt could overshoot the debt reduction target for euro zone members. \par \par \uc2  Strauss-Kahn said on Thursday France will meet or even beat its 1999 deficit target of 2.3 percent of gross domestic issue. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said France's admission that it may overshoot the debt target would further undermine the credibility of the euro, after Italy said in May it would not meet its declared budget deficit goal of 2.0 percent of GDP. \par \par \uc2  "This (French) story has further to run," said Crise. \par \par \uc2  The euro took some comfort from a rise in the Ifo business climate index for western Germany to 90.4 in May from 89.7 in April, beating economists' forecasts and market rumours circulating on Wednesday, traders said. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chief Economist Otmar Issing said the Ifo survey was an encouraging sign for the euro-zone economy. The ECB left interest rates unchanged on Thursday, as expected. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen traded within a one-yen range on Thursday, with any declines in dollar/yen or euro/yen limited by fear of further dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , following similar moves on Monday and last week. \par \par \uc2  "If it weakens much more, we know the Japanese will step in and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Brian Hilliard, chief economist UK at Societe Generale. \par \par \uc2  But economic fundamentals favoured the yen, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Trade Minister Kaoru Yosano said he thought Japan's GDP for the April-June quarter would show growth from the previous quarter. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei average ended at its highest level since October, 1997, and Japanese bond yields rose, an incentive to Japanese investors to keep their money at home. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 119.77/80 yen at 1524 GMT, from 120.22/27 in late European trade on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling recovered from Wednesday's two-month lows against the dollar, boosted by stronger than expected growth in British retail sales in May of 1.0 percent month-on-month. \par \par \uc2  The pound was trading at $1.5928/33 from Wednesday's low of $1.5854 and from $1.5855/65 late in Europe on Wednesday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of Greece helped the euro to stabilise against the drachma, which has gradually appreciated following the end of NATO attacks against Yugoslavia. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Greek \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought 170-200 million euros for drachmas at its daily fixing session. \par \par \uc2  Euro/drachma traded near its lows at 323.30/40, compared with a high reached during the Kosovo conflict of 331.40, set on May 7. \par \par \uc2  ((Carolyn Cohn, London Capital Markets +44 171 542 6320, carolyn.cohn@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6h0666v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
