\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Dollar ventures below 120 yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 496 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 1999\par 13:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 17 (Reuters) - The dollar ventured below 120 yen in European trading on Thursday as rising Japanese bond yields and a rally in the Nikkei overnight offset some of the lingering wariness about Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The euro was firmer against the dollar, recovering some of Wednesday's losses with the help of a mildly supportive reading in Germany's Ifo business climate data. \par \par \uc2  "We saw maybe up to $14 billion of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, but we're already back here," said Minoru Ozawa, head of trading at Sakura Bank in London. "It suggests there are quite a few people who still need to sell dollars." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 119.87/97 yen at 1110 GMT, down from 120.22/27 late in Wednesday's European trading. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears had kept the dollar pinned above 120 yen in the last few days, but the dollar appeared to have a downward bias in the absence of an actual BOJ action, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "As long as it's relatively stable, the BOJ will more likely look at what the best timing is to make a move, rather than look at any levels around here," Ozawa said. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market did not have strong directional momentum ahead of Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's testimony before the Joint Economic Committee at 1400 GMT. \par \par \uc2  "If Greenspan has strong words on inflation, it's a done deal," a European bank trader said, referring to the chances of an interest rate hike at the Federal Open Market Committee meeting scheduled for June 29-30. \par \par \uc2  He noted that Greenspan in recent years had given tacit warnings to the market before interest rate moves. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to cut interest rates at its ongoing governing council meeting, but that interest rate differentials continued to favour the dollar against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Although the Ifo figures were somewhat better than expected, it would take time for evidence to build up that the European economy was on a clear growth track, they said. \par \par \uc2  Ifo business climate index for western Germany in May came in at 90.4, a notch above an average forecast of 90.3, compared with April's 89.7. \par \par \uc2  Sterling also firmed on positive economic news, pulling back from two-month lows against the dollar with the help of stronger-than-expected U.K. retail sales data. \par \par \uc2  May retail sales were up 1.0 percent from a month earlier versus expectations of a 0.6 percent rise. \par \par \uc2  The pound rose nearly a cent from Wednesday's $1.5854 low at one point before stabilising at around $1.5910/15. Euro/sterling was little changed, hugging the 65.00 pence level. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, Denmark's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut the repo rate to 2.85 percent from 2.90 pct, citing the crown's strength. ((Shin Kishima, London Capital Markets +44-171-542-6721)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6h065gh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
