\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr shelters in BOJ shadow, awaits inflation test. \b0\par\par\uc2 754 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 1999\par 05:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 16 (Reuters) - The dollar was a shade firmer against the yen at Wednesday's midsession, helped by the threat of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but again shied away from seriously testing offshore offers around 120.70 yen. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the shadow of the Bank of Japan still lay long on the market and many harboured suspicions the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was using public funds to protect 120.20 support with bids. \par \par \uc2  But all attempts at building a rally had been foiled by selling from U.S. funds, Japanese exporters and speculators betting that U.S. inflation data out later in the day would roil financial markets and send the dollar sliding again. \par \par \uc2  By 0415 GMT the dollar was pinned at 120.48/54 yen, having held to a tiny 20-tick range all morning after finishing at 120.35/45 in New York on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Chart support was seen at 120.10 and 119.65, and there was talk of stop-loss offers building around the latter. A break of Monday's 120.90 high was needed to give the dollar its own momentum and allow a test of major resistance around 121.35. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had been briefly aided by talk the BOJ might leave the surplus generated by Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. That would have depressed the yen by signalling an even looser policy approaching a quantitative easing. \par \par \uc2  But in the event the BOJ sterilised the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , draining 1.3 trillion yen of liquidity and leaving only one trillion, around the daily average of recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  That removed one potential threat to dollar bears, but the risk of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was real enough to keep sellers at bay for the moment. \par \par \uc2  That could change today depending on what implications the May U.S. consumer price index has for monetary policy there. \par \par \uc2  It was a shock 0.7 percent rise in inflation in April that convinced the market the Federal Reserve needed to tighten and it has since fully discounted a 25 basis point hike. \par \par \uc2  The median forecast is for a modest rise of 0.2 percent in the headline and core CPI. Such a gain would suggest April's strength was an aberration and support speculation the Fed may only have to hike once or maybe twice this cycle. \par \par \uc2  At the same time, analysts say a rise of 0.4 percent or more would provoke serious market worries that the Fed was behind the curve on inflation and could see a renewed flight out of U.S. asset markets, thus hurting the dollar. \par \par \uc2  On this reasoning, a very weak CPI would tend to aid the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  even if it meant U.S. rates, and thus the dollar's yield differential, would not rise as far as assumed. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the euro was also on the sidelines ahead of the CPI, sitting at $1.0429/39 and hardly budging from New York's late $1.0422/29. \par \par \uc2  Traders said there was talk of persistent Japanese selling of euro/yen for hedging purposes, which was also serving to keep the yen supported against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The euro was stuck at 125.62/71 yen compared with 125.55 late in New York. \par \par \uc2  Sterling had been the main mover overnight, sliding more than one cent after surprisingly soft U.K. inflation data stirred talk the Bank of England might ease again. \par \par \uc2  That prompted the money market to sharply scale back its expectations for future tightening and caused the spread of 10-year gilts under Treasuries to widen to more than 100 basis points for the first time ever. \par \par \uc2  This was woeful timing for bulls who had loaded up with pounds on the belief last week's BoE rate cut was the last of the cycle. They were forced to bail out, leaving sterling down at $1.5940/50 at midsession from a high of $1.6085 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The dive ruined a promising rally and left the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  looking vulnerable from a technical perspective, with the breach of last week's $1.5968 low considered a very bearish omen. \par \par \uc2  Analysts now expect resistance around $1.6010, if not $1.5975, to contain any corrective bounce before a test of Tuesday's $1.5900 trough and a possible break to support at $1.5875, which shields April's critical low at $1.5838. ((Wayne Cole, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8584 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6g06dsr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
