\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar/yen trade less volatile-Japan MOF's Kuroda. \b0\par\par\uc2 177 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 1999\par 02:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 16 (Reuters) - Senior Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Haruhiko Kuroda said on Wednesday that dollar/yen trading has become less volatile but he declined to say whether the market has stabilised after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Asked if the dollar's movement around 120 yen after Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  meant the market had stabilised, Kuroda, the Finance Ministry's International Bureau chief, told Reuters: "I won't comment on market levels, but it is a fact that the range has narrowed. \par \par \uc2  "We want to continue closely monitoring the market." \par \par \uc2  Asked if the authorities' resolve to curb the yen's rise had permeated the market, Kuroda said: "I cannot comment on the causes of market movements, but \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability is very important for the timing of (Japan's) economic recovery." \par \par \uc2  Kuroda declined comment on whether he thought the effect of the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue. ((Hirotaka Kogure, Tokyo newsroom +81-3 3432-8022 email: tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6g069vd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
