\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - BOJ fears inhibit yen bulls. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,218 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 1999\par 17:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 15 (Reuters) - The dollar was trapped in its tightest ranges against the yen since April as fears of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept it pinned above 120 yen on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers remained nervous a day after the BOJ's second \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in five days, particularly since Japanese officials said overnight the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would step in again if needed. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of Japan made fairly clear that they do not want dollar/yen to fall," said a trader at a U.S. bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "There was talk that they might already step in if the yen dips below 119.50 yen, but they seem more likely to have drawn a line in the sand at 118 yen." \par \par \uc2  By 1500 GMT the dollar was at 120.58/68 yen from Tuesday's peak of 120.71 yen and within the half-yen range that had caged it throughout the European session. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, the dollar had jumped a quarter-yen within minutes. While the move was sparked by U.S. investment banks' buying, London dealers said the speed with which other banks leapt to buy dollars highlighted nervousness after Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Analysts said speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic recovery was not yet guaranteed despite last week's strong gross domestic product numbers was creeping into the market and also giving the dollar some support against the yen. \par \par \uc2  "We have to see whether the GDP numbers were a one-off or the beginning of a trend," said Jeff Woddruff, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of Boston in London. \par \par \uc2  "My suspicion is that this is not a trend." \par \par \uc2  A 3.4 percent fall in Japan's April revised industrial output data after a 2.7 percent rise in March seemed to confirm the view that Japan's economy had not yet turned the corner. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also benefitting from expectations that a rise in May U.S. consumer price index, due for release on Wednesday, would add to the case for an increase in U.S. interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "The big story is U.S. monetary policy and tomorow will likely confirm that they are likely to be raising rates," said Kit Juckes, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at NatWest GFM. "How much that supports the dollar is the principal story out there now." \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile the euro staged a late recovery above $1.04 from one-week lows hit earlier, capitalising on the dollar's retreat from one-week highs against the Swiss franc, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the euro's recent rally was losing steam. \par \par \uc2  "It is beginning to show slightly alarming signs of renewed weakness," said Dick Howard, director of economic research at Julius Baer Investments in London. \par \par \uc2  A 1.4-percent month-on-month decrease in Italy's April industrial output, which was wider than the 0.8 percent fall economists had forecasted, was giving euro bears a reason to probe the downside, he added. \par \par \uc2  Sterling fell to 2-1/2 week lows against the dollar after British May inflation came in weaker than expected, undershooting the government's 2.5 percent target. \par \par \uc2  The data fuelled speculation that UK interest rates might be cut again. \par \par \uc2  The pound was at $1.5962/72 against $1.6087/92 late on Monday. Euro/sterling was at 65.25/26 pence versus 64.76/78. \par \par \uc2  UK Chancellor of the Exchequer Gordon Brown told a parliamentary committee that Britain had no intention of rejoining the European exchange rate mechanism and any decision to join the euro would be based on national interests. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in Europe the dollar eased from one-week highs of 1.5348 set earlier against the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  Swiss National Bank Vice Chairman Jean-Pierre Roth told Reuters he was unimpressed by the franc's swings against the dollar and that gains the dollar had racked up earlier this month were not a big concern for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  ((Lisa Jucca, London Capital Markets +44-171-542-6745, elisabetta.jucca@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  By 1500 GMT the dollar was at 120.58/68 yen from Tuesday's peak of 120.71 yen and within the half-yen range that had caged it throughout the European session. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, the dollar had jumped a quarter-yen within minutes. While the move was sparked by U.S. investment banks' buying, London dealers said the speed with which other banks leapt to buy dollars highlighted nervousness after Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Analysts said speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic recovery was not yet guaranteed despite last week's strong gross domestic product numbers was creeping into the market and also giving the dollar some support against the yen. \par \par \uc2  "We have to see whether the GDP numbers were a one-off or the beginning of a trend," said Jeff Woddruff, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of Boston in London. \par \par \uc2  "My suspicion is that this is not a trend." \par \par \uc2  A 3.4 percent fall in Japan's April revised industrial output data after a 2.7 percent rise in March seemed to confirm the view that Japan's economy had not yet turned the corner. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also benefitting from expectations that a rise in May U.S. consumer price index, due for release on Wednesday, would a