\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar labours despite promise of official help. \b0\par\par\uc2 615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 1999\par 07:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 15 (Reuters) - The dollar bowed before underlying selling pressure by Tuesday midday, even though it had the threat of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) on its side. \par \par \uc2  Traders said there had been no overt sign of the BOJ so far today, although some suspected a Japanese public fund or two might be on the bid, and the market brushed off a torrent of dollar-supportive comment from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar also won a brief safe-haven gain on news that South Korean ships exchanged fire with vessels from the North, but offers again proved too heavy above 120.60 yen. By 0300 GMT, the dollar had fallen to 120.35/40 yen from an early 120.67 high. \par \par \uc2  That was only slightly down from the late Monday level of 120.65/68 and a long way above that day's 117.87 low, but dealers were concerned the BOJ's money had not had more of an effect. \par \par \uc2  "They're supposed to have spent over $8.0 billion to get the dollar above 120 and that's a big chunk of change in anyone's book," said a senior dealer at a U.S. bank. \par \par \uc2  "But it hasn't changed the dollar's trend. There are still offers from 120.50 right up to 122.00, and that's a natural demand-supply disparity which the BOJ is struggling with," he said, referring to the fact that Japan is running a huge trade surplus and the United States a massive deficit. \par \par \uc2  The disparity did not matter so much when Japanese and other offshore investors were willing to recycle their surpluses into U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  But with investors increasingly nervous over the U.S. inflation and policy outlook, the flow of funds has been slowing if not reversing, so putting pressure on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "If the BOJ decides to stand in the way of this, it had better be ready to spend a lot more money," the trader added.< \par \par \uc2  The first major tests would come on Wednesday with the release of U.S. consumer price data for May, and on Thursday when Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan testifies. \par \par \uc2  It was April's shock 0.7 percent jump in CPI which convinced the market the Fed would have to tighten rates soon, so analysts are anxious to see if the May figures suggest it was an aberration or confirm a worsening trend. \par \par \uc2  Median forecasts are for a rise of 0.2 percent and they fear a rise of 0.4 percent or more would pressure asset markets and thus the dollar. \par \par \uc2  So pervasive has the fear of inflation become that many think a rate rise would actually calm the market and reinforce confidence in the Fed's credibility. Thus most analysts are looking for a tightening at the FOMC meeting at the end of June. \par \par \uc2  For the moment, dealers suspected the BOJ would act again if the dollar slid beneath 120.10 yen support, so this level was likely to be the floor for the near term. \par \par \uc2  Stiff resistance was seen around 120.60 and 120.90 while the dollar would have to clear trendline resistance around 121.35 to win back market favour. \par \par \uc2  The euro was easier, having been dumped offshore by investors disappointed at its repeated failure to build a break above $1.0500. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was idling at $1.0423/33 at midsession having found support at $1.0410, after slipping from $1.0477/82 in late Tokyo on Monday. \par \par \uc2  ((Wayne Cole, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8584 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)) (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6f06gzm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
