\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CROSS-Euro/yen directionless as market eyes BOJ. \b0\par\par\uc2 505 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 1999\par 07:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 15 (Reuters) - The euro hovered narrowly against the yen in early afternoon on Tuesday in Tokyo cross trade, directionless as the market was on alert against possible dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ). \par \par \uc2  "The euro's upside is capped as both Japanese and overseas players are still eager to sell near 126 yen," said a dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at 125.48/60 yen at 0434 GMT, compared with 125.65 yen in New York late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Overseas fund operators were unwinding their euro-long, yen-short positions, while Japanese investors were still looking to sell euro for hedging purposes amid expectations that the yen had room to gain further in the near term, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  A bullish mood for the yen was fuelled by a robust 7.9 percent annualised growth rate shown in Japan's January-March gross domestic product data released last week. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the euro/yen's direction may largely depend on the dollar/yen rate, which was trapped in a tight range as the market focused on Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  On Monday in Tokyo, the BOJ repeatedly stepped into the market to buy dollars, pushing the greenback as high as 120.90 yen from a low of 117.87 yen. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no trace of BOJ buying so far on Tuesday, but expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to step in again if 120 yen was threatened. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 120.41/44 yen, little changed from 120.53/58 yen in New York late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Hank Note, senior vice president at Sumitomo Bank, said: "Yesterday, the BOJ appeared to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively even above 120 yen. So 120 yen seems to be the lowest level the authorities are willing to tolerate for the time being." \par \par \uc2  Selling interest from Japanese institutional investors and overseas hedge funds is strong in the upper end of the 120-121 yen range and above 121 yen, Note said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese financial authorities on Tuesday reiterated their warnings against excessive yen strength, but their remarks had limited impact on the market. \par \par \uc2  Haruhiko Kuroda, head of the Finance Ministry's International Bureau, told Reuters earlier on Tuesday that there was no change in his view that an excessively strong yen would be bad for Japan's economy. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said on Tuesday that the ministry would take steps against any "destructive factors" in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara, who on Monday confirmed the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , told reporters on Tuesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again if necessary to prevent an excessive yen rise. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Swiss franc was at 78.62/67 yen, compared with 78.82 yen in New York late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at 193.39/51 yen, versus 193.67 yen in New York. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk 81-3 3432 9780 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6f06fio\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
