\par\par \b \uc2  OPTIONS - Dollar/yen vols off as spot treads water. \b0\par\par\uc2 574 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 1999\par 00:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CHICAGO, June 15 (Reuters) - Implied volatilities for dollar/yen options eroded further on Tuesday, with the dollar lodged above 120 yen after Monday's aggressive Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  boosted the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  One-month volatility fell to 11.0/11.3 percent, down from 11.7/12.0 percent on Monday and the peak last week of 13.1/13.5 percent. The one-year declined to 12.8/13.0 percent from Monday's 13.25/13.35 percent and 13.7/13.85 percent last week. \par \par \uc2  "Many people have gotten out of their long vega and long gamma positions, which brought the whole curve lower from where it was last week," said Masao Okada, chief options dealer for Dai-Ichi Kangyo Bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  With the market fearful of selling dollar/yen after the BOJ displayed its resolve to curtail yen strength, the dollar sat in a half-yen range Tuesday, finishing the U.S. session slightly weaker at 120.38 yen. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's second \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in five days lifted the dollar three yen from a low near 117.85 yen, and Japanese officials said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would act again if needed. \par \par \uc2  "People were very worried about the downside, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  started to calm things down," said an options dealer for a Canadian bank. \par \par \uc2  "There were some pretty big sellers (of volatility). It's almost like they knew there was going to be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  even before the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Risk reversals in one-month 25-delta dollar/yen options saw their dollar downside bias moderate Tuesday to 1.0/1.3 in favour of the yen calls, in from 1.3/1.5 on Monday and last week's 1.7/1.9 extreme. \par \par \uc2  Okada said the market will closely watch BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday, when \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade settlements occur, to see whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  absorbs funds supplied to the banking system via the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The BOJ on Monday was said to have bought between $5 billion and $10 billion for yen, which would have resulted in the injection of some one trillion yen into the banking system. \par \par \uc2  If the BOJ refrains from draining the excess funds, it would constitute a "quantitative easing," Okada said. \par \par \uc2  "If the market regards the BOJ action as a message to the market that the BOJ is going to take up a quantitative easing to strengthen the economy, this will push up the Nikkei stock market and people will not hesitate to sell yen aggressively," Okada said. \par \par \uc2  Euro/dollar volatilities were little changed at 9.4/9.6 percent in the one-month and 8.8/8.9 percent in the one-year with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hovering around $1.04 most of the session. \par \par \uc2  The euro call bias that materialised on last week's euro rebound, inspired by the Kosovo peace settlement, faded Tuesday to a scant flat/0.2 bias for the calls. \par \par \uc2  With the Kosovo conflict over, the euro is likely to resume its downtrend against the dollar, especially if the U.S. Federal Reserve raises interest rates at its end-of-the-month policy meeting, traders said. \par \par \uc2  But the euro should now decline at a slower pace than it did during the war, keeping volatility at bay, traders said. \par \par \uc2  ((Susan Kelly, Chicago Derivatives Desk 312-408