\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Debt Mixed Midday; Bond Futures, 10-Yr Bonds Up \b0\par\par\uc2 435 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 1999\par 05:14\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese government bonds prices are mixed midday Monday, with long-term bonds mostly higher after a technical bounce prompted dealers to unload short positions by buying bond futures. \par \par \uc2  Traders said few expect the Bank of Japan to alter its zero interest-rate policy at a board meeting, scheduled to conclude later in the session. This is despite speculation late last week that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might raise interest rates sooner than expected, following the release of surprisingly robust January to March domestic gross domestic product figures. \par \par \uc2  Traders attributed bond market gains at the start of trading to the yen's sudden jump against the dollar. But news that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market weakened the yen and boosted stocks, prompting "speculative accounts to sell bond futures and buy stock futures," said one trader at a Japanese securities firm. \par \par \uc2  That helped pushed the lead September 10-year bond futures down to a midmorning low of 130.79. \par \par \uc2  By the midday break, however, most long-term bond prices were higher. \par \par \uc2  A second trader at a Japanese securities firm said some dealers unwound short bond positions in Tokyo after bond futures rebounded from technical support at 130.79. \par \par \uc2  While there is some talk that bond futures are poised to rise to 132.50, "there isn't much buying by investors to support such a rise," said the second trader. \par \par \uc2  The September 10-year bond futures contract closed the morning session at 131.47, up 0.60, and the benchmark 10-year bond yield was at 1.665%, down from 1.75% last Friday. \par \par \uc2  Trading in the March 2000 10-year bond futures contract, which began last Friday, was relatively quiet. There is talk, however, that a paucity of cheapest-to-deliver cash bonds needed to deliver the September contract is likely to cause an exodus to the March 2000 contract in coming weeks, said one swaps trader at a German bank. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Nikkei-225 stock index finished the morning session at 17305.55,\par up 107 from Friday.\par    Tiffe Mar 2000 3-Mo Euroyen Yld:  0.38%   Dn 0.04\par    Simex Mar 3-Mo 2000 Euroyen Yld:  0.38%   Dn 0.01\par    TSE Sep 5-Yr JGB Future Price:    116.48  Dn 0.31\par    TSE Sep 10-Yr JGB Future Price:   131.47  Up 0.60\par    Simex Sep 10-Yr JGB Future Price: 130.50  Dn 0.30\par    10-Yr 1.4% JGB No. 213 Yld:       1.665%  Dn 0.085\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  06-13-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2314EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1037 4153 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010826dv6e011hi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
