\par\par \b \uc2  MOF Kuroda/Steps -2:Will Take More Steps To Stem Yen Rise \b0\par\par\uc2 194 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 1999\par 03:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--A top official at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Monday that the Japanese financial authorities took necessary action against an excessively strong yen and said that Japan would continue to take measures to stem the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  Speaking to reporters, Haruhiko Kuroda, the head of the international bureau, declined to comment on the level at which the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar Monday morning. \par \par \uc2  "We took the necessary steps in the market," Kuroda said. "We will take measures against an excessively strong yen in the future." \par \par \uc2  After the dollar fell as low as Y117.91 in early Tokyo trade Monday, it suddenly jumped above Y119 amid rumors that the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers noted that the BOJ apparently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market - the first time around Y118.20-30 and the second time around Y118.80-90. Speculation was that the BOJ may have spent $1 billion-$2 billion on in its latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Around 0154 GMT, the dollar is quoted around Y119.90. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 14-06-99 \par \par \uc2  0157GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv6e04hs2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
