\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Sakakibara: To Curb Yen Rise, Keep Rates Low -FT \b0\par\par\uc2 154 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 1999\par 01:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Eisuke Sakakibara, vice minister for international affairs at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said in an interview with the Financial Times that the government will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to curb any sharp rise of the yen following last week's strong gross domestic product figures, and is determined to keep interest rates low. \par \par \uc2  Quoted in the Tuesday edition of the FT, Sakakibara said "Too strong a yen is not desirable. We would like to halt any premature appreciation." \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara also confirmed that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday to stem the yen's rise for the second time in three trading days. Following the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yen weakened to close at about Y120 against the dollar in Tokyo, about Y3 weaker than in late trading in New York last Friday. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 14-06-99 \par \par \uc2  2357GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv6e04g81\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
