\par\par \b \uc2  MOF Kuroda says steps taken in  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  market Monday. \b0\par\par\uc2 188 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 1999\par 03:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 14 (Reuters) - Senior Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) official Haruhiko Kuroda said that Japan had taken steps in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Monday as excessive yen strength was unfavourable for the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  Asked about Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ), which senior Finance Ministry official Eisuke Sakakibara confirmed earlier in the day, Kuroda said: "Excessive strength in the yen is not favourable for the Japenese economy, so we took steps in the market." \par \par \uc2  But Kuroda, director-general of the ministry's International Bureau, said he could not comment on the levels at which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  took place. \par \par \uc2  Asked what the ministry would do next, Kuroda said: "We will always take appropriate steps against an excessively strong yen." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept up the pace of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday morning, buying dollars for yen and helping to push the dollar above 120 yen by mid-morning trade, up from an earlier low of 117.45 yen. ((Yoko Nishikawa, Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6e06lqk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
