\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar jumps nearly three yen on BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 767 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 1999\par 09:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 14 (Reuters) - The dollar jumped nearly three yen at one point on Monday from its intraday low after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars for yen and Japanese officials spoke out against excessive rises in the yen. \par \par \uc2  Dollar-buying by the BOJ was confirmed by senior Finance Ministry officials, who said Japan took action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent excessive strengthening of the yen. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the total amount of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BOJ could be as much as $5.0 billion or even more. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 120.30/35 yen as of 0629 GMT, up sharply from 117.88/98 yen in late New York on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose to an intraday high of around 120.80 yen earlier, up nearly three yen from its intraday low of 117.87 yen. \par \par \uc2  Judging from the strong wording used by Japanese officials on Monday, dealers said it was possible that European central banks or the U.S. Federal Reserve may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars on behalf of the BOJ in overseas markets later on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Eisuke Sakakibara, vice finance minister for international affairs, earlier on Monday said: "As I have said, in order to ensure the Japanese economy recovers, we will prevent excessive strength in the yen. We will continue to monitor \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves and take decisive action if necessary." \par \par \uc2  Separately, senior MOF official Haruhiko Kuroda said on Monday: "Excessive strength in the yen is not favourable for the Japanese economy, so we took steps in the market." \par \par \uc2  Asked what the ministry would do next, Kuroda said: "We will always take appropriate steps against an excessively strong yen." \par \par \uc2  Both Sakakibara and Kuroda declined to comment at what levels the BOJ's dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  took place. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the BOJ started buying dollars on Monday morning at 118.20 yen, with some bankers saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have started buying from 117.90 yen. The BOJ was detected buying dollars repeatedly, even at above 120 yen. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was detected continuing into late Tokyo trading, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen buying the greenback at the upper end of the 120-121 yen range. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the latest dollar-buying by the BOJ at around 0700 GMT pushed the dollar to another intraday high of around 120.90 yen. \par \par \uc2  But they said selling pressure against the dollar was so strong that without the BOJ dollar-buying the greenback could have quickly fallen towards 115 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's sentiment is incredibly bad. Developments such as the recent sell-off in U.S. bond markets have left the dollar very susceptible to selling pressure," a U.S. bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Sellers on Monday included Japanese exporters and institutional investors as well as overseas fund operators. \par \par \uc2  "We saw a significant amount of dollar-selling at levels above 120 yen by Japanese exporters as well as by some Japanese institutional investors," said a senior options trader for a major city bank. \par \par \uc2  Overseas fund operators were also detected selling dollars. Dealers said their selling may have been caused by the unwinding of yen carry trades by overseas hedge funds. \par \par \uc2  In the near term, dealers said the dollar was likely to be supported near 120 yen by concerns about more BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I think we could see dollar buying by foreign central banks on behalf of the BOJ in overseas markets if the dollar falls back below 120 yen," said Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for Morgan Stanley Bank AG. \par \par \uc2  He said in any case Japanese authorities were likely to prevent the dollar from falling below 116.12 yen, the average dollar/yen rate quoted by Japanese firms for setting up their business plans for the current business year in the BOJ's most recent "tankan" survey. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the euro stood at $1.0496/01 as of 0724 GMT, compared with $1.0528/33 yen in late New York on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's Policy Board said late on Monday, in a widely anticipated move, that it had decided to keep monetary policy unchanged by majority vote. \par \par \uc2  An annualised 7.9 percent rise in Japan's gross domestic product in the January-march quarter had raised concerns that the BOJ may end its policy of guiding short-term interest rates to near zero earlier than expected, but money traders have said that such a move could still be about a year away. ((Masayuki Kitano, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 5473 3748 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6e06l8j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
