\par\par \b \uc2  Yen in retreat as BOJ battles to turn the tide. \b0\par\par\uc2 554 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 1999\par 06:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 14 (Reuters) - The yen was in full retreat at a chaotic midsession on Monday after the Bank of Japan waded into the market buying dollars in the billions. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  guru Eisuke Sakakibara threw down the gauntlet during the morning with an unusually emphatic declaration that the MOF would not allow any premature appreciation in the yen. \par \par \uc2  The words were backed with action as the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  not just once but repeatedly - rumour had the amount spent at $5.0 billion and over. That sped the dollar all the way to 120.47/53 yen from an early low of 117.87. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was accompanied by a chorus of official encouragement, with Vice Finance Minister Koji Tanami and senior official Haruhido Kuroda echoing the line that an excessive yen rise was unfavourable for Japan. \par \par \uc2  But traders said the BOJ was not having it all its own way, with European and U.S. funds believed to be dumping their long dollar positions. Some hedge funds were thought to be exiting yen carry trades, which have soured now that U.S. asset markets are under pressure. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, dealers thought the BOJ's endeavours may come to naught if U.S. markets extended their slide this week. \par \par \uc2  "They (the BOJ) have been in non-stop and are clearly serious," said one dealer at a U.S. bank. "But many in the market are happy to take the oportunity to sell the dollar at better levels." \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ has failed to instil fear in the market and until it does so, this effort is doomed," he said, adding that one reason for the bears' confidence was that the Federal Reserve was not expected to join the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "If the Fed came in, everything would change and we'd see the dollar at 122.50 or higher in a hurry, but this is not considered a likely scenario," he said. \par \par \uc2  He noted that the dollar's rally had stalled at the 120.60 chart barrier and there was further resistance up at 121.35. \par \par \uc2  Support was now seen around 120.10 and it would need a breach of 119.50 to revive the downtrend towards 117.63. \par \par \uc2  Fallout from the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  extended right across the market, with the yen fleeing almost everywhere while the dollar won a broad boost. \par \par \uc2  The euro jumped on the yen to stand at 126.18/27 yen by 0400 GMT from 123.92/02 early oin the morning. It faltered short of major chart resistance at 126.45 yen, the high from June 9, but analysts were bullish for a push to 127.66. \par \par \uc2  The flows into dollar/yen weighed on the euro/dollar cross, keeping it pinned at $1.0489/94 compared with $1.0528/33 late in New York on Friday. \par \par \uc2  But support at $1.0465 had held solid so far and analysts were generally positive following last week's bounce from record lows around $1.0260. A break of Friday's $1.0558 peak was needed to confirm the improving trend and open the way to $1.0645, the top from May 25. ((Wayne Cole, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8584 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6e06knr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
