\par\par \b \uc2  Job steps Y500 bln budget enough for now-Keidanren. \b0\par\par\uc2 247 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 1999\par 10:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 14 (Reuters) - An expected extra budget of 500 billion yen for a jobs package announced by the Japanese government should be enough for now, the head of business lobby Keidanren (Federation of Economic Organisations), Takashi Imai, told a news conference on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "We just had tax cuts and we are also feeling the impact of the continuation of a large volume of public works spending. Therefore, we think that an extra budget of 500 billion yen is sufficient for now," Imai said. \par \par \uc2  On recent market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an apparent bid to stop the yen's appreciation, Imai said that a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate of 120 yen to the dollar was "a comfortable level" for many Japanese companies. \par \par \uc2  The Keidanren chief also said the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should maintain its "zero interest-rate" policy. \par \par \uc2  Even given Thursday's strong gross domestic product data for the January-March quarter, Imai said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should maintain its current accommodative policy as the data was not a great cause for optimism. \par \par \uc2  The government plans soon to unveil an extra budget to finance job-creation measures. \par \par \uc2  The package featured steps to help firms scrap the so-called "three excesses" of overcapacity, mounting debt and bloated payrolls, while curbing rising unemployment and helping people move to new jobs. ((Tokyo Equities Desk +81-3 3432 9404 tokyo.equities.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokumen